window.LESSONS=[{"id":"r5-psalm139","type":"Poetry & Wisdom","title":"O LORD, You Have Searched Me and Known Me","ref":"Psalm 139:1–24","round":5,"parts":[{"id":"s1","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 1","range":"Psalm 139:1–12","verses":[{"ref":"Psalm 139:1","n":1,"text":"For the choirmaster. A Psalm of David. O LORD, You have searched me and known me."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:2","n":2,"text":"You know when I sit and when I rise; You understand my thoughts from afar."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:3","n":3,"text":"You search out my path and my lying down; You are aware of all my ways."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:4","n":4,"text":"Even before a word is on my tongue, You know all about it, O LORD."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:5","n":5,"text":"You hem me in behind and before; You have laid Your hand upon me."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:6","n":6,"text":"Such knowledge is too wonderful for me, too lofty for me to attain."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:7","n":7,"text":"Where can I go to escape Your Spirit? Where can I flee from Your presence?"},{"ref":"Psalm 139:8","n":8,"text":"If I ascend to the heavens, You are there; if I make my bed in Sheol, You are there."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:9","n":9,"text":"If I rise on the wings of the dawn, if I settle by the farthest sea,"},{"ref":"Psalm 139:10","n":10,"text":"even there Your hand will guide me; Your right hand will hold me fast."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:11","n":11,"text":"If I say, “Surely the darkness will hide me, and the light become night around me”—"},{"ref":"Psalm 139:12","n":12,"text":"even the darkness is not dark to You, but the night shines like the day, for darkness is as light to You."}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"This is a {{psalm|psalm}} of David, for the {{choirmaster|choirmaster}}, and David is talking straight to the LORD. He opens with this: \"O LORD, You have searched me and known me.\" Searched AND known, friend, like somebody went through my old junk drawer and knew every rubber band by name.","ref":"Psalm 139:1"},{"t":"choice","q":"David says the LORD understands his thoughts, but only when David is close by.","ref":"Psalm 139:2","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["I get it, friend, because that's exactly how it works with me. If you're not standing right next to my oven, I have NO idea what you're thinking. But David tells the LORD in Psalm 139:2, \"You understand my thoughts from afar.\" Distance doesn't slow the LORD down one bit.","Distance means nothing to the LORD. David puts it to Him plainly in Psalm 139:2: \"You understand my thoughts from afar.\" Close up, far away, it makes no difference to Him. Me, friend, I lose track of my own thoughts from across the bakery."]},{"t":"story","text":"From {{afar|afar}}, friend! The LORD knows when David sits and when he rises, and I can't even remember where I set my timer.","ref":"Psalm 139:2"},{"t":"choice","q":"David says the LORD searches out his path and his lying down, and is aware of ___ his ways.","ref":"Psalm 139:3","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["all","some","most"],"correct":0,"expl":["Every last one of them. The LORD searches out David's path and his lying down, and then comes the big line in Psalm 139:3: \"You are aware of all my ways.\" Walking, resting, everything in between. Friend, I'm aware of maybe three of my ways, and one of them is losing my timer.","Some would already be impressive, friend, I'll give you that. But David goes all the way. Here's his line in Psalm 139:3: \"You are aware of all my ways.\" Not a sample batch, not a handful, the WHOLE tray.","So close it hurts, friend. Most is how I describe my good batches. But the line David gives in Psalm 139:3 is bigger than that: \"You are aware of all my ways.\" The LORD doesn't have a \"mostly\" setting."]},{"t":"story","text":"ALL his ways, friend, every step AND every nap. Then David goes even further and talks about the words that come out of his mouth.","ref":"Psalm 139:3–4"},{"t":"choice","q":"About the words David speaks, when does he say the LORD knows all about them?","ref":"Psalm 139:4","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Before a word is on his tongue","Once the words are spoken","After he writes them down"],"correct":0,"expl":["Early, early, EARLY. In Psalm 139:4, David prays, \"Even before a word is on my tongue, You know all about it, O LORD.\" The word hasn't even left the station yet and the LORD already knows all about it. Me, friend, I find out what I'm saying about the same time everybody else does.","That's how the rest of us find out, friend. Somebody talks, then we know. But the LORD is way earlier than that. David says in Psalm 139:4, \"Even before a word is on my tongue, You know all about it, O LORD.\"","Friend, the LORD doesn't need a screenshot from the group chat. In Psalm 139:4, David says the LORD knows all about a word even before it's on his tongue. Before it's said, before it's typed, before ANYTHING. I write my cookie recipes down and STILL forget them."]},{"t":"story","text":"The LORD {{hems|hems}} David in, behind and before, and David says, \"{{idiom:534|You have laid Your hand upon me}}.\" That's me packing a cookie box, mustache on both sides, NOTHING slipping out.","ref":"Psalm 139:5"},{"t":"choice","q":"The LORD knows David's every move and every word. What does David say about knowledge like that?","ref":"Psalm 139:6","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["It's too wonderful for him to reach","It's easy enough for him to explain","It's something he figured out long ago"],"correct":0,"expl":["That's right where David lands. He tells the LORD in Psalm 139:6, \"Such knowledge is too wonderful for me, too lofty for me to attain.\" I picture the man just standing there, looking up, speechless. Same, David, same. I've been baking for ages and I still can't explain how my oven knows when to beep.","Not even close, friend. David throws his hands up on this one. Look at Psalm 139:6: \"Such knowledge is too wonderful for me, too lofty for me to attain.\" Too high to reach. Picture me trying to grab the top shelf of the bakery with a mustache that only stretches so far.","He hasn't figured it out at ALL, friend, and he says so. In Psalm 139:6, David calls this knowledge \"too wonderful for me, too lofty for me to attain.\" That's not a man with it all worked out. That's a man looking up and going, WOW."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then David asks, \"Where can I go to escape Your Spirit? Where can I {{flee|flee}} from Your presence?\" Friend, I spent years at the back of a junk drawer, and I STILL got found.","ref":"Psalm 139:7"},{"t":"choice","q":"David says that if he went up to the heavens the LORD would be there, but if he made his bed in {{Sheol|Sheol}} he'd finally be alone.","ref":"Psalm 139:8","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["Sheol sure sounds like a good spot to be left alone, friend. But David says in Psalm 139:8, \"If I ascend to the heavens, You are there; if I make my bed in Sheol, You are there.\" The heavens or Sheol, same answer both times. Wherever David makes his bed, the LORD is there.","No alone time in Sheol either, friend. David covers both ends in Psalm 139:8: \"If I ascend to the heavens, You are there; if I make my bed in Sheol, You are there.\" Two places, and both times, You are there. Believe me, I spent years at the back of a junk drawer, and I STILL got found."]},{"t":"story","text":"Heavens or {{Sheol|Sheol}}, the LORD is there both times. What if David rose on the wings of the dawn, or settled by the farthest sea?","ref":"Psalm 139:8–9"},{"t":"choice","q":"Way out by the farthest sea, what does David say the LORD's hand will do there?","ref":"Psalm 139:10","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Guide him and hold him fast","Push him back toward home","Let go and let him wander"],"correct":0,"expl":["Way out by the farthest sea, and the LORD's hand is still right on David. Here's how he says it in Psalm 139:10: \"even there Your hand will guide me; Your right hand will hold me fast.\" That's a GRIP. Friend, I can't even hold onto a cookie box when somebody sneezes.","No shove back to the shore here, friend. In Psalm 139:10, David prays, \"even there Your hand will guide me; Your right hand will hold me fast.\" Right there, way out by the farthest sea, the LORD guides him and holds on. Nobody gets pushed anywhere.","It's the total opposite, friend. Way out by the farthest sea, David tells the LORD, \"Your right hand will hold me fast.\" That's Psalm 139:10, and that same hand guides him too. Think of me with a fresh tray of cookies. I am NOT letting go."]},{"t":"story","text":"One more hiding spot. What if David said the darkness would surely hide him, and the light would become night around him?","ref":"Psalm 139:11"},{"t":"choice","q":"David says that even the darkness is not dark to the LORD.","ref":"Psalm 139:12","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["Not even a little dark, not to the LORD. Look at Psalm 139:12, where David says \"even the darkness is not dark to You\" and that settles it. The hiding spot he just pictured doesn't hide a thing. Me, friend, I turned off the bakery lights once to sneak a cookie and walked face first into the oven.","I hear you, friend, dark usually means hidden. That's why I do all my secret cookie taste tests with the lights off. But in Psalm 139:12, David says \"even the darkness is not dark to You\" about the LORD. The darkness David pictured hiding him doesn't hide a thing from Him."]},{"t":"story","text":"In fact, \"the night shines like the day, for darkness is as light to You.\" Meanwhile I can't find my apron with the lights ON.","ref":"Psalm 139:12"}]},{"id":"s2","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 2","range":"Psalm 139:13–24","verses":[{"ref":"Psalm 139:13","n":13,"text":"For You formed my inmost being; You knit me together in my mother’s womb."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:14","n":14,"text":"I praise You, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Marvelous are Your works, and I know this very well."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:15","n":15,"text":"My frame was not hidden from You when I was made in secret, when I was woven together in the depths of the earth."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:16","n":16,"text":"Your eyes saw my unformed body; all my days were written in Your book and ordained for me before one of them came to be."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:17","n":17,"text":"How precious to me are Your thoughts, O God, how vast is their sum!"},{"ref":"Psalm 139:18","n":18,"text":"If I were to count them, they would outnumber the grains of sand; and when I awake, I am still with You."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:19","n":19,"text":"O God, that You would slay the wicked— away from me, you bloodthirsty men—"},{"ref":"Psalm 139:20","n":20,"text":"who speak of You deceitfully; Your enemies take Your name in vain."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:21","n":21,"text":"Do I not hate those who hate You, O LORD, and detest those who rise against You?"},{"ref":"Psalm 139:22","n":22,"text":"I hate them with perfect hatred; I count them as my enemies."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:23","n":23,"text":"Search me, O God, and know my heart; test me and know my concerns."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:24","n":24,"text":"See if there is any offensive way in me; lead me in the way everlasting."}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Now David goes back to the very beginning: \"For You formed my {{inmost being|inmost being}}; You knit me together in my mother’s {{womb|womb}}.\" Knit, friend, stitch by stitch, and I couldn't stamp one straight cookie for YEARS.","ref":"Psalm 139:13"},{"t":"choice","q":"Thinking about how the LORD made him, what does David do?","ref":"Psalm 139:14","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["He praises the LORD","He asks the LORD why","He keeps quiet about it"],"correct":0,"expl":["Straight to praise! Thinking about how the LORD knit him together, David says in Psalm 139:14, \"I praise You, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.\" Out comes worship. Friend, when I look back at my early stamps, praise is NOT the first word that comes out.","Asking why is a very human move, friend. I ask my oven why every single morning. But David goes another way here. He says in Psalm 139:14, \"I praise You, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.\"","Keeping it to himself is not David's style here, friend. He puts it right into Psalm 139:14: \"I praise You, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.\" And the whole psalm is for the choirmaster, no less (Psalm 139:1). That's the opposite of keeping quiet."]},{"t":"story","text":"David calls himself {{fearfully|fearfully}} and wonderfully made, and he adds, \"Marvelous are Your works, and I know this very well.\" WONDERFULLY made, friend, while my first batch of stamps came out looking like somebody sat on them.","ref":"Psalm 139:14"},{"t":"choice","q":"David says that while he was made in secret, woven together in the {{depths of the earth|depths of the earth}}, his {{frame|frame}} was hidden from the LORD.","ref":"Psalm 139:15","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["Made in secret sure sounds hidden, friend, I'll give you that. But David says in Psalm 139:15, \"My frame was not hidden from You when I was made in secret, when I was woven together in the depths of the earth.\" Secret to everybody else, but not to the LORD. Every bit of David's frame was in plain view for Him.","Not hidden at all. In Psalm 139:15, David says his frame \"was not hidden from You\" even while he was made in secret and woven together in the depths of the earth. Secret, deep down, and STILL in plain view. Meanwhile, friend, I hide one cookie behind the cookie boxes and can't find it myself the next day."]},{"t":"story","text":"Before David was even formed, the LORD's eyes saw his {{unformed body|unformed body}}. Me? I can't tell what a batch is gonna look like till it comes out of the oven, friend.","ref":"Psalm 139:16"},{"t":"choice","q":"David says all his days were written in the LORD's book and {{ordained|ordained}} for him ___ one of them came to be.","ref":"Psalm 139:16","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["before","after","once"],"correct":0,"expl":["Every one of David's days was on the page before day one. He puts it this way in Psalm 139:16: \"all my days were written in Your book and ordained for me before one of them came to be.\" Not one day had happened yet, and they were already written. Friend, I can't even plan tomorrow's batch until I've hit snooze twice.","After would make the LORD a great record keeper, friend, but David goes way earlier than that. In Psalm 139:16 he says his days were \"written in Your book and ordained for me before one of them came to be.\" Written ahead of time, every one of them. Not a diary, friend, a plan.","Once is close, friend, but David reaches back even further. In Psalm 139:16 he says his days were ordained for him \"before one of them came to be.\" Before day one, they were already in the LORD's book. That's like having the whole cookie box packed before I've stamped a single cookie."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then David says, \"How precious to me are Your thoughts, O God, how {{vast|vast}} is their {{sum|sum}}!\" Friend, I've got about two thoughts most mornings, and one of them is the oven timer.","ref":"Psalm 139:17"},{"t":"choice","q":"David says he could count up God's thoughts if he only had enough time.","ref":"Psalm 139:18","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["More time sounds like it would do the trick, friend. But in Psalm 139:18, David says that if he tried to count God's thoughts, they would outnumber the grains of sand. That's not a time problem. That's a sand problem. I tried counting the sprinkles in one jar and quit before noon.","No clock in the world fixes this one, friend. Psalm 139:18 is where David says it: \"If I were to count them, they would outnumber the grains of sand; and when I awake, I am still with You.\" I lose count of the chocolate chips in ONE batch. He's talking about SAND."]},{"t":"story","text":"If he tried, David says, they would outnumber the grains of sand, \"and when I awake, I am still with You.\"","ref":"Psalm 139:18"},{"t":"story","text":"Then the {{psalm|psalm}} takes a hard turn. David wishes God would {{slay|slay}} the {{wicked|wicked}}, and he tells {{bloodthirsty|bloodthirsty}} men to get away from him.","ref":"Psalm 139:19"},{"t":"choice","q":"David wants those {{bloodthirsty|bloodthirsty}} men away from him. How does he describe them?","ref":"Psalm 139:20","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["As God's enemies","As his old friends","As foreign soldiers"],"correct":0,"expl":["God's enemies, plain and simple. In Psalm 139:20, David says these men \"speak of You deceitfully; Your enemies take Your name in vain.\" They misuse God's own name. It's a heavy turn in the psalm, friend, and David doesn't soften it.","There's no history like that in these verses, friend. The name David gives them in Psalm 139:20 is a lot heavier: \"Your enemies take Your name in vain.\" They speak of God deceitfully. Nothing about a falling out over a borrowed cookie box.","I can see why soldiers came to mind, friend, with all that talk of bloodthirsty men. But David never says where they're from. In Psalm 139:20 he calls them God's enemies, people who \"speak of You deceitfully\" and who take God's name in vain. What he points to is how they treat God's name."]},{"t":"story","text":"David doesn't tiptoe here, friend. He asks the LORD, \"Do I not hate those who hate You, O LORD, and {{detest|detest}} those who {{rise against|rise against}} You?\"","ref":"Psalm 139:21"},{"t":"choice","q":"David asks if he doesn't hate the people who hate the LORD. How does he answer his own question?","ref":"Psalm 139:21–22","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["He hates them as his enemies","He forgives them freely","He says he pities them"],"correct":0,"expl":["David answers his own question with no wiggle room, friend. In Psalm 139:22 he says, \"I hate them with perfect hatred; I count them as my enemies.\" The people who hate the LORD, he counts as his own enemies. Those are strong words, and he says them right to God.","Forgiveness doesn't come up in this line, friend. What David says in Psalm 139:22 is, \"I hate them with perfect hatred; I count them as my enemies.\" The LORD's enemies become David's enemies. He's standing with the LORD against the people who hate Him.","Pity would be gentler, friend, but David doesn't go there. His words in Psalm 139:22 are \"I hate them with perfect hatred; I count them as my enemies.\" Perfect hatred is about as far from pity as it gets."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then, friend, the whole {{psalm|psalm}} swings back around. David stops talking about God's enemies and starts praying about HIMSELF.","ref":"Psalm 139:23"},{"t":"choice","q":"Praying about himself now, David asks God to search him and know his heart.","ref":"Psalm 139:23","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["Yep, David flings the door wide open. In Psalm 139:23 he prays, \"Search me, O God, and know my heart; test me and know my concerns.\" He's inviting the search himself. Friend, I won't even let anybody peek in my oven early, and David is opening up his whole heart.","He really does ask, friend, and that's the twist. In Psalm 139:23 David prays, \"Search me, O God, and know my heart\" right after all that talk about God's enemies. Most of us would keep the messy parts out of sight, like a baker shoving the burnt batch to the back of the oven before the inspector walks in. David says, come look."]},{"t":"story","text":"David prays, \"Search me, O God, and know my heart; test me and know my concerns.\" He opened by saying the LORD had searched him, and now he's ASKING for it, like a baker calling the inspector back on purpose.","ref":"Psalm 139:23"},{"t":"choice","q":"David asks God to see if there is any {{offensive|offensive}} way in him. He also asks God to ___ him in the way {{everlasting|everlasting}}.","ref":"Psalm 139:24","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["lead","leave","hide"],"correct":0,"expl":["Lead it is, and what a way to close out a psalm. David's last request, in Psalm 139:24, goes like this: \"See if there is any offensive way in me; lead me in the way everlasting.\" First he asks God to look, then he asks God to lead. Not a map, friend. A Guide.","Leave looks a lot like lead, friend, but they go in opposite directions. Back in Psalm 139:7, David asks where he could even flee from God's presence. Then in Psalm 139:24, he doesn't ask God to leave at all. He prays, \"lead me in the way everlasting.\" I know a thing or two about getting left in a drawer, and trust me, lead is the one you want.","Hiding doesn't work in this psalm, friend. Earlier, even the darkness can't hide David from the LORD (Psalm 139:11-12). And the request that closes the psalm, in Psalm 139:24, is \"lead me in the way everlasting.\" Honestly, after years behind the takeout menus, I'd pick being led too."]}]},{"id":"b","kind":"burnt","title":"Burnt Cookies","intro":"One last retelling, friend. Two of these cookies stayed in my oven too long, so each one has a detail that's wrong. Tap the 2 burnt ones.","lines":[{"text":"David opens with, \"O LORD, You have searched me and known me.\" The LORD knows when he sits, when he rises, and every word BEFORE it hits his tongue. Too wonderful for David to reach, friend.","ref":"Psalm 139:1–6","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Where can David flee from God's presence? Up to the heavens, down in Sheol, rising on the wings of eagles, out by the farthest sea. The LORD is there EVERY time, and even the darkness is as light to Him.","ref":"Psalm 139:7–12","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"rising on the wings of eagles","true":"rising on the wings of the dawn","ref":"Psalm 139:9"}]},{"text":"The LORD knit David together in his mother's womb, so David praises Him: fearfully and wonderfully made! And all his days were written in God's book before one of them came to be.","ref":"Psalm 139:13–16","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"God's thoughts are precious and vast. If David tried to count them, they'd outnumber the stars in the sky, and when he wakes up, he's still with God.","ref":"Psalm 139:17–18","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"the stars in the sky","true":"the grains of sand","ref":"Psalm 139:18"}]},{"text":"David wants the bloodthirsty men away from him and counts God's enemies as his own. Then he turns the search on HIMSELF: \"Search me, O God, and know my heart; test me and know my concerns.\" Then he asks God to lead him in the way everlasting.","ref":"Psalm 139:19–24","burnt":false,"wrong":[]}]},{"id":"e","kind":"ending","title":"The Big Idea","lesson":"","bigIdea":["Here's the whole psalm in one bite, friend: there's NOWHERE David can go that God isn't already there, and NOTHING about him God doesn't already know, not his sitting, not his lying down, not his words before he says them (Ps 139:1-12).","And that's not a trap, it's a comfort. Wherever David ends up, God's hand is there to guide him and hold him fast (Ps 139:10). I lose my apron in my OWN kitchen, and David can't find ONE spot God isn't.","God knows David that well because He MADE him. Every one of David's days was already in God's book before the first one began (Ps 139:13-16).","I think being known that well could feel scary. David calls it \"too wonderful for me\" (Ps 139:6), and he says, \"How precious to me are Your thoughts, O God\" (Ps 139:17).","Then David turns to the wicked. He asks God to slay them, calls them bloodthirsty men, and says they are God's enemies who take His name in vain (Ps 139:19-20). He says he hates those who hate God: \"I hate them with perfect hatred; I count them as my enemies\" (Ps 139:21-22).","But he doesn't end pointing at them. He ends pointing at HIMSELF: \"Search me, O God, and know my heart\" (Ps 139:23). Friend, most folks won't even let somebody scroll through their phone. David asks God to search his HEART.","So the God who searched David at the start (Ps 139:1) is the God David invites in at the end, asking Him to \"lead me in the way everlasting\" (Ps 139:24)."]}],"words":{"psalm":{"word":"psalm","meaning":"A song or poem of prayer and praise to God. The Book of Psalms collects 150 of them, and many were sung in worship.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"choirmaster":{"word":"choirmaster","meaning":"The leader of the singers and musicians in worship. A note like \"for the choirmaster\" says the song was handed to him to be sung.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)","Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"afar":{"word":"afar","meaning":"Far away. \"From afar\" means from a long way off.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"hems":{"word":"hems","meaning":"To hem someone in is to close them in on every side so there is no way out. Here it pictures God surrounding David all around.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"flee":{"word":"flee","meaning":"To run away quickly, usually to get away from something.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Sheol":{"word":"Sheol","meaning":"The Hebrew name for the place of the dead, thought of as deep below the earth. In this psalm it stands for the farthest place from the heavens.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"fast":{"word":"fast","meaning":"Here it means firmly or tightly, not quickly. To hold someone fast is to keep a tight grip so they do not slip away.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"inmost being":{"word":"inmost being","meaning":"The deepest, innermost part of a person. The Hebrew word is literally \"kidneys,\" which people then spoke of as the hidden center of a person's feelings and thoughts.","sources":["BSB footnote"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"womb":{"word":"womb","meaning":"The place inside a mother's body where a baby grows before it is born.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"fearfully":{"word":"fearfully","meaning":"Here it does not mean scared. It means in a way that fills you with awe, so amazing that it makes you stand in wonder.","sources":["Strong's Concordance (H3372)","Brown-Driver-Briggs Hebrew Lexicon"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"frame":{"word":"frame","meaning":"The body's build, its bones and structure. Here David means his whole body as it was being formed.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)","Strong's Concordance (H6108)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"depths of the earth":{"word":"depths of the earth","meaning":"A poetic picture of a deep, hidden place out of sight. David uses it for the hidden place where he was formed before birth.","sources":["Matthew Henry's Commentary","Barnes' Notes on the Bible"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"unformed body":{"word":"unformed body","meaning":"A body not yet finished being made. The Hebrew word points to a baby in its very earliest stage, before it has its full shape.","sources":["Strong's Concordance (H1564)","Brown-Driver-Briggs Hebrew Lexicon"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"ordained":{"word":"ordained","meaning":"Planned and set in place ahead of time. David means God had already shaped and settled each of his days.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"vast":{"word":"vast","meaning":"Huge, so big it is hard to measure.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"sum":{"word":"sum","meaning":"The total you get when you add everything up. David means God's thoughts add up to a huge number.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"slay":{"word":"slay","meaning":"To kill, often in a violent way or in battle.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"wicked":{"word":"wicked","meaning":"Truly evil people who do wrong on purpose and turn against God.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"bloodthirsty":{"word":"bloodthirsty","meaning":"Eager to hurt or kill people, wanting violence.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"detest":{"word":"detest","meaning":"To hate something very strongly, to find it disgusting.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"rise against":{"word":"rise against","meaning":"To rebel against someone or stand up to fight them.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"pities":{"word":"pities","meaning":"Feels sorry for someone who is suffering or in a bad spot.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"offensive":{"word":"offensive","meaning":"Here it means a hurtful or wrong way of living that displeases God. The Hebrew word carries the idea of pain or grief, and is also linked with idols.","sources":["Strong's Concordance (H6090)","Brown-Driver-Briggs Hebrew Lexicon"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"everlasting":{"word":"everlasting","meaning":"Lasting forever, without ever ending.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"idiom:534":{"word":"You have laid Your hand upon me","meaning":"God holds the psalmist in His grip; he is always close at hand and under God's control, held up and held back, with no way to escape Him.","sources":["Barnes","Gill","Cambridge","Pulpit","Keil and Delitzsch"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"},"idiom:535":{"word":"if I make my bed in Sheol","meaning":"Sheol is the underworld or grave, the lowest place, set against heaven, the highest. Even if the psalmist lay down in the depths of the dead, God would be there. No place is beyond God's presence.","sources":["Ellicott","Barnes","Gill","Pulpit","Matthew Henry"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"}}},{"id":"r5-isaiah55","type":"Prophetic","title":"Come, All You Who Are Thirsty","ref":"Isaiah 55:1–13","round":5,"parts":[{"id":"s1","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 1","range":"Isaiah 55:1–5","verses":[{"ref":"Isaiah 55:1","n":1,"text":"“Come, all you who are thirsty, come to the waters; and you without money, come, buy, and eat! Come, buy wine and milk without money and without cost!"},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:2","n":2,"text":"Why spend money on that which is not bread, and your labor on that which does not satisfy? Listen carefully to Me, and eat what is good, and your soul will delight in the richest of foods."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:3","n":3,"text":"Incline your ear and come to Me; listen, so that your soul may live. I will make with you an everlasting covenant — My loving devotion promised to David."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:4","n":4,"text":"Behold, I have made him a witness to the nations, a leader and commander of the peoples."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:5","n":5,"text":"Surely you will summon a nation you do not know, and nations who do not know you will run to you. For the LORD your God, the Holy One of Israel, has bestowed glory on you.”"}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"This is the LORD speaking in Isaiah, and He calls out an invitation: \"Come, all you who are thirsty, come to the waters; and you without money, come, buy, and eat!\" No money, baker? FINALLY, a store my wallet can handle.","ref":"Isaiah 55:1"},{"t":"choice","q":"The LORD offers wine and milk too, and He says they come without money and without ___.","ref":"Isaiah 55:1","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["cost","delay","end"],"correct":0,"expl":["Two withouts, one right after the other! Isaiah 55:1 offers wine and milk \"without money and without cost!\" That's not a sale, that's a giveaway.","Delay? Nope, this blank is all about the price. Isaiah 55:1 calls out to the thirsty, then to \"you without money,\" and the LORD offers wine and milk with no money and no cost at all. Free, free, and then FREE again. My kind of checkout line.","Ooh, \"without end\" has a nice ring to it, I'll give you that. But this verse is talking price tags, and there isn't one. Wine and milk, no money, no cost, all in Isaiah 55:1. If I ran my bakery like that, my cash register would get lonely."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then He asks why spend money on what is not bread, and {{labor|labor}} on what does not satisfy. Baker, that's me at midnight ordering a fourth oven timer I will never use.","ref":"Isaiah 55:2"},{"t":"choice","q":"Instead of chasing what doesn't satisfy, the LORD tells them to listen carefully to Him.","ref":"Isaiah 55:2","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["Isaiah 55:2 asks why spend money on what is not bread, and then the LORD gives the fix: \"Listen carefully to Me, and eat what is good, and your soul will delight in the richest of foods.\" Quit chasing what leaves you empty and come listen. It's like scrolling for an hour looking for something good when the good thing was sitting right there the whole time.","Look again, this one's actually true. The LORD doesn't stop at asking why they labor on what does not satisfy. He tells them what to do instead, and His very next words in Isaiah 55:2 tell them to listen carefully to Him. Do that, and their soul will delight in the richest of foods. The fix for empty isn't trying harder. It's listening to Him."]},{"t":"story","text":"Do that, He says, and eat what is good, and their soul will delight in the richest of foods. Then the LORD says, \"{{incline|Incline}} your ear and come to Me; listen, so that your soul may live.\"","ref":"Isaiah 55:2–3"},{"t":"story","text":"And He makes a promise: an {{everlasting|everlasting}} {{covenant|covenant}} with them. He ties it to His {{loving devotion|loving devotion}}, promised to one man by name.","ref":"Isaiah 55:3"},{"t":"choice","q":"The LORD ties this {{everlasting|everlasting}} {{covenant|covenant}} to His {{loving devotion|loving devotion}} promised to which man?","ref":"Isaiah 55:3","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["David","Moses","Abraham"],"correct":0,"expl":["David, Israel's king, is the man named in Isaiah 55:3, where the LORD speaks of \"My loving devotion promised to David.\" Verse 4 keeps going about him: \"Behold, I have made him a witness to the nations, a leader and commander of the peoples.\" The LORD put a name on this promise, and it's David's. Me, I only got my name carved on a handle.","Not Moses this time. Moses, Israel's leader, never comes up in this passage. In Isaiah 55:3 the LORD ties the covenant to \"My loving devotion promised to David.\" That's David, Israel's king. Moses will have to sit in the back for this one, and trust me, I know all about sitting in the back.","Here's the twist: Abraham, the forefather of Israel, isn't named anywhere in this passage. The name in Isaiah 55:3 belongs to David, Israel's king, because the LORD speaks of His loving devotion promised to David. Then verse 4 stays right on him, calling him a witness to the nations. One name on this promise, and it's David's."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then the LORD says He made David a {{witness|witness}} to the nations. And He tells the people He's inviting that nations who don't know them will RUN to them, and baker, I don't run for anything but the oven timer.","ref":"Isaiah 55:4–5"},{"t":"choice","q":"Besides a {{witness|witness}} to the nations, the LORD says He made David a leader and commander of the peoples.","ref":"Isaiah 55:4","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["Witness, leader, AND commander. In Isaiah 55:4 the LORD says, \"Behold, I have made him a witness to the nations, a leader and commander of the peoples.\" Three titles! Meanwhile my title is \"guy who packs boxes with his face.\"","Easy to miss, because the witness part comes first. But in Isaiah 55:4 the LORD keeps going: \"a leader and commander of the peoples.\" So David gets the whole set, witness, leader and commander. That is a LOT of name tags for one shirt."]},{"t":"choice","q":"The LORD tells the people He's inviting that nations who don't know them will run to them. Why would those nations come running?","ref":"Isaiah 55:5","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Their God has made them glorious","Their armies have made them strong","Their trade has made them rich"],"correct":0,"expl":["Isaiah 55:5 gives the reason right there: \"For the LORD your God, the Holy One of Israel, has bestowed glory on you.\" Look at that word \"bestowed.\" The glory is a gift from God, and because of it, nations come RUNNING to this people. Me, I only run for the oven timer.","Armies get a nation noticed, sure. But Isaiah 55:5 doesn't mention a single soldier. The nations come running because the LORD their God, the Holy One of Israel, has bestowed glory on them. That's God's gift, not anybody's muscle.","Money talks, I know, but not in this verse. Isaiah 55:5 says the draw is glory, and it's the LORD their God, the Holy One of Israel, who gave it to them. Funny thing is, this whole chapter opened back in verse 1 with buying \"without money and without cost!\" Riches were never the point here."]}]},{"id":"s2","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 2","range":"Isaiah 55:6–13","verses":[{"ref":"Isaiah 55:6","n":6,"text":"Seek the LORD while He may be found; call on Him while He is near."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:7","n":7,"text":"Let the wicked man forsake his way and the unrighteous man his thoughts; let him return to the LORD, that He may have compassion, and to our God, for He will freely pardon."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:8","n":8,"text":"“For My thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways My ways,” declares the LORD."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:9","n":9,"text":"“For as the heavens are higher than the earth, so My ways are higher than your ways and My thoughts than your thoughts."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:10","n":10,"text":"For just as rain and snow fall from heaven and do not return without watering the earth, making it bud and sprout, and providing seed to sow and food to eat,"},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:11","n":11,"text":"so My word that proceeds from My mouth will not return to Me empty, but it will accomplish what I please, and it will prosper where I send it."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:12","n":12,"text":"You will indeed go out with joy and be led forth in peace; the mountains and hills will burst into song before you, and all the trees of the field will clap their hands."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:13","n":13,"text":"Instead of the thornbush, the cypress will grow, and instead of the brier, the myrtle will spring up; this will make a name for the LORD, an everlasting sign, never to be destroyed.”"}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Now the voice shifts, and a call goes out: \"Seek the LORD while He may be found; call on Him while He is near.\" Friend, this is not a snooze button kind of invitation.","ref":"Isaiah 55:6"},{"t":"story","text":"The call goes on: let the {{wicked|wicked}} man {{forsake|forsake}} his way and the {{unrighteous|unrighteous}} man his thoughts, and let him return to the LORD.","ref":"Isaiah 55:7"},{"t":"choice","q":"When the {{wicked|wicked}} man returns to the LORD, the LORD will freely {{pardon|pardon}} him.","ref":"Isaiah 55:7","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["No fine print on this one. Isaiah 55:7 says to return to the LORD, \"that He may have compassion, and to our God, for He will freely pardon.\" FREELY. No processing fee. No \"please hold, your pardon is important to us.\"","Too good to be true? Not here. Isaiah 55:7 ends with \"for He will freely pardon.\" It calls the wicked man to forsake his way and return to the LORD, \"that He may have compassion,\" and that's a promise worth coming home to. Believe me, an old stamper who spent years behind the takeout menus knows how good it feels when somebody reaches in for you."]},{"t":"story","text":"And then: \"For My thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways My ways,\" declares the LORD. Baker, I can't even keep up with my own thoughts, and I only have one bakery to run.","ref":"Isaiah 55:8"},{"t":"choice","q":"The LORD says that just as the heavens are above the earth, His ways and thoughts are ___ than theirs.","ref":"Isaiah 55:9","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["higher","older","simpler"],"correct":0,"expl":["Think about how far the sky is above your head. That's the gap the LORD describes in Isaiah 55:9: \"For as the heavens are higher than the earth, so My ways are higher than your ways and My thoughts than your thoughts.\" Me, I can barely reach the top shelf over my ovens.","Older sounds wise, but the picture here is all about height. The LORD measures it against the sky: \"as the heavens are higher than the earth\" (Isaiah 55:9). His ways and thoughts sit that far above ours. Look up, way up, and you've got it.","Ha! Simpler would be nice, wouldn't it? But in Isaiah 55:9 the LORD says His ways and thoughts are higher than theirs, just as the heavens are higher than the earth. Higher, WAY up there. Good thing, too, because I once spent twenty minutes looking for the spatula I was holding with my own mustache."]},{"t":"story","text":"Next comes a picture: rain and snow fall from heaven, and they don't go back without watering the earth. The earth buds and sprouts, and there's seed to {{sow|sow}} and food to eat.","ref":"Isaiah 55:10"},{"t":"choice","q":"Rain and snow don't return to heaven without watering the earth. The LORD says something of His works the same way. What is it?","ref":"Isaiah 55:10–11","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["The word from His mouth","The tears of His people","The blessing on their fields"],"correct":0,"expl":["The LORD finishes the picture in Isaiah 55:11: \"so My word that proceeds from My mouth will not return to Me empty, but it will accomplish what I please, and it will prosper where I send it.\" Rain comes down, the earth buds and sprouts, and the job gets done. His word works just like that.","Tears don't show up anywhere in this passage, friend. The rain picture points to something the LORD sends out, and in Isaiah 55:11 it's the word that proceeds from His mouth. Rain doesn't go back without watering the earth, and His word won't come back empty either. Every drop counts.","So close, because rain and fields really do go together! But the thing being compared comes in Isaiah 55:11, where the LORD says the word that proceeds from His mouth will not return to Him empty. The rain is the picture. His word is the point."]},{"t":"choice","q":"The LORD says His word will go out and come back to Him empty.","ref":"Isaiah 55:11","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["Other way around! Empty is the one thing His word will NOT do. The LORD says it \"will not return to Me empty, but it will accomplish what I please\" (Isaiah 55:11). Me, I send a batch into the oven and sometimes it comes back as charcoal. His word? Never.","His word goes out and gets the job done. In the LORD's own words in Isaiah 55:11, it will \"accomplish what I please, and it will prosper where I send it.\" Not empty. Not halfway. I wish my first thousand cookie stamps had that kind of track record."]},{"t":"story","text":"Not a chance: His word will accomplish what He pleases and {{prosper|prosper}} where He sends it. And the LORD tells them they will indeed go out with joy and be {{led forth|led forth}} in peace.","ref":"Isaiah 55:11–12"},{"t":"choice","q":"As they go out with joy, what does the LORD say the mountains and hills will do before them?","ref":"Isaiah 55:12","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Burst into song","Tremble and quake","Melt like wax"],"correct":0,"expl":["Mountains. Singing. In Isaiah 55:12 the LORD says, \"You will indeed go out with joy and be led forth in peace; the mountains and hills will burst into song before you, and all the trees of the field will clap their hands.\" I can't carry a tune past the mixing bowl, and here come the HILLS harmonizing.","Not this time! This is a joyful parade. In Isaiah 55:12 the LORD says they will go out with joy and be led forth in peace, and the mountains and hills will \"burst into song\" before them. No shaking here, just a hillside choir.","Melting is a cookie problem, friend, not a mountain problem, at least not here. In Isaiah 55:12 the LORD says the mountains and hills will \"burst into song\" as they go out with joy. This whole scene is joy and peace. Let's leave the melting to my chocolate chips."]},{"t":"story","text":"And all the trees of the field will clap their hands. Friend, I pack cookie boxes with my MUSTACHE, so clapping trees sounds totally normal to me.","ref":"Isaiah 55:12"},{"t":"choice","q":"In this joyful picture, {{thornbush|thornbushes}} and {{brier|briers}} spread across the land.","ref":"Isaiah 55:13","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["Flip it! In Isaiah 55:13 the LORD says that instead of the thornbush, the cypress will grow, and instead of the brier, the myrtle will spring up. The thorns are on their way OUT. It's like tossing a burnt batch and pulling a perfect one out of the oven.","Out with the prickly stuff! In Isaiah 55:13 the thornbush and the brier get replaced. The cypress grows where the thornbush was, and the myrtle springs up where the brier was. Honestly, that's the most satisfying swap since my crooked stamps finally came out straight."]},{"t":"story","text":"Instead of the {{thornbush|thornbush}}, the {{cypress|cypress}} will grow, and instead of the {{brier|brier}}, the {{myrtle|myrtle}} will spring up. And this will make a name for the LORD.","ref":"Isaiah 55:13"},{"t":"choice","q":"The LORD says the {{cypress|cypress}} growing where the {{thornbush|thornbush}} was, and the {{myrtle|myrtle}} where the {{brier|brier}} was, will be a sign that is ___.","ref":"Isaiah 55:13","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["everlasting","temporary","hidden"],"correct":0,"expl":["Cypress where the thornbush was, myrtle where the brier was, and Isaiah 55:13 says this will make a name for the LORD that lasts. It's \"an everlasting sign, never to be destroyed.\" Every cookie I've ever stamped is long gone. This sign sticks around FOREVER.","Oh, this one goes the long way! In Isaiah 55:13 the LORD calls it \"an everlasting sign, never to be destroyed.\" Temporary is a plate of my cookies sitting in front of me. This sign lasts forever.","Hidden? Not this sign! Picture cypress and myrtle growing right where the thornbush and brier used to be. Isaiah 55:13 says this will make a name for the LORD. That's not a secret. That's a sign."]}]},{"id":"b","kind":"burnt","title":"Burnt Cookies","intro":"One last retelling, friend. Two of these cookies stayed in my oven too long, so each one has a detail that's wrong. Tap the 2 burnt ones.","lines":[{"text":"The LORD calls out to everyone who's thirsty: come to the waters, and if you've got no money, come, buy, and eat! Wine and milk without cost, baker. Then He asks why spend money on what is not bread.","ref":"Isaiah 55:1–2","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"He promises an everlasting covenant, His loving devotion promised to Solomon. He made him a witness to the nations, and nations who don't know His people will RUN to them.","ref":"Isaiah 55:3–5","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"His loving devotion promised to Solomon","true":"His loving devotion promised to David","ref":"Isaiah 55:3"}]},{"text":"Then a call goes out: seek the LORD while He may be found. Let the wicked man forsake his way and return, for He will freely pardon. And the LORD declares His ways are higher than theirs, as the heavens are higher than the earth.","ref":"Isaiah 55:6–9","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Rain and snow fall from heaven and don't go back without watering the earth. Same with the word from His mouth: it won't come back to Him empty. Baker, that's more than I can say for my cookie jar.","ref":"Isaiah 55:10–11","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"They'll go out with joy and be led forth in peace. The mountains and hills will burst into song, and the rivers will clap their hands. And where the thornbush was, the cypress will grow.","ref":"Isaiah 55:12–13","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"the rivers will clap their hands","true":"all the trees of the field will clap their hands","ref":"Isaiah 55:12"}]}]},{"id":"e","kind":"ending","title":"The Big Idea","lesson":"","bigIdea":["Baker, Isaiah 55 is the LORD throwing the doors wide open and calling, COME. Thirsty? Come. No money? Come anyway. What He offers is free (Isa 55:1).","And what He offers is the thing that actually satisfies. People keep spending money and work on what doesn't (Isa 55:2). That's paying for a gym membership every month and only ever using the parking lot.","The real feast is listening to Him. The LORD says, \"listen, so that your soul may live.\" Then He backs it with an everlasting covenant, His loving devotion promised to David (Isa 55:3).","And it gets bigger. He made David a witness to the nations, and nations who don't know the people He's inviting will RUN to them, because the LORD has bestowed glory on them (Isa 55:4-5). Total strangers, whole nations, sprinting over. That's not a crowd, that's a STAMPEDE.","So answer while the door's open: \"Seek the LORD while He may be found;\" let the wicked man forsake his way and the unrighteous man his thoughts and return to the LORD, and He will freely pardon (Isa 55:6-7). FREELY. Same price tag as the water!","Sound too good to be true? Not for Him. His thoughts and ways are higher than ours, as high as the heavens over the earth (Isa 55:8-9), and His word never comes back empty. Like rain and snow, it does what He sends it to do (Isa 55:10-11). I send one message to a group chat and get back... NOTHING. His word? Never.","It all ends in joy and peace, with singing mountains, clapping trees and cypress where the thornbush was, a name for the LORD that lasts forever (Isa 55:12-13). That's the whole chapter, friend: the LORD Himself is what satisfies, it costs you nothing, and nations will come running to the people He's inviting. So come thirsty, come broke, just COME."]}],"words":{"labor":{"word":"labor","meaning":"Hard work, or the effort and earnings a person puts into something.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"incline":{"word":"incline","meaning":"To bend or lean toward something. To incline your ear means to lean in and pay close attention.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"everlasting":{"word":"everlasting","meaning":"Lasting forever; never coming to an end.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"covenant":{"word":"covenant","meaning":"A solemn, binding agreement between two sides. In the Bible, it is often a promise God makes and commits Himself to keep with His people.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary","Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"loving devotion":{"word":"loving devotion","meaning":"God's faithful, loyal love: kindness that sticks with the people He has promised Himself to and does not give up on them.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"witness":{"word":"witness","meaning":"Someone who tells others what is true from what they have seen or know. Here, David shows the nations who God is.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"wicked":{"word":"wicked","meaning":"Evil; deliberately doing what is wrong and against God's ways.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"forsake":{"word":"forsake","meaning":"To leave something behind for good and turn away from it.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"unrighteous":{"word":"unrighteous","meaning":"Not right with God; living in a way that is unjust or sinful.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"pardon":{"word":"pardon","meaning":"To forgive someone and let them go free from the punishment their wrong deserves.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"sow":{"word":"sow","meaning":"To plant seed by scattering it on the ground so it can grow into a crop.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"prosper":{"word":"prosper","meaning":"To succeed and turn out well; to get done what it was meant to do.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"led forth":{"word":"led forth","meaning":"Guided out and onward, the way a leader walks people out of one place toward another.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"thornbush":{"word":"thornbush","meaning":"A shrub covered in sharp thorns that grows on poor, neglected ground and makes land hard to use.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"brier":{"word":"brier","meaning":"A prickly, thorny plant or bramble, often a sign of wild, unworked land.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary","Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"cypress":{"word":"cypress","meaning":"A tall evergreen tree. The Hebrew word may also mean a pine, juniper, or fir.","sources":["BSB footnote","Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"myrtle":{"word":"myrtle","meaning":"An evergreen shrub with shiny leaves, sweet-smelling white flowers, and berries, common in the land of Israel.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary","Smith's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"idiom:433":{"word":"incline Your ear","meaning":"To incline the ear is to bend it toward someone and listen closely. It asks for, or trusts in, God's attentive hearing and answering of prayer, even the quiet sighs of the oppressed.","sources":["Benson","Barnes","Gill","Keil and Delitzsch"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"}}},{"id":"r5-luke6","type":"Sermons","title":"Jesus Teaches on a Level Place","ref":"Luke 6:17–49","round":5,"parts":[{"id":"s1","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 1","range":"Luke 6:17–28","verses":[{"ref":"Luke 6:17","n":17,"text":"Then Jesus came down with them and stood on a level place. A large crowd of His disciples was there, along with a great number of people from all over Judea, Jerusalem, and the seacoast of Tyre and Sidon."},{"ref":"Luke 6:18","n":18,"text":"They had come to hear Him and to be healed of their diseases, and those troubled by unclean spirits were healed."},{"ref":"Luke 6:19","n":19,"text":"The entire crowd was trying to touch Him, because power was coming from Him and healing them all."},{"ref":"Luke 6:20","n":20,"text":"Looking up at His disciples, Jesus said: “Blessed are you who are poor, for yours is the kingdom of God."},{"ref":"Luke 6:21","n":21,"text":"Blessed are you who hunger now, for you will be filled. Blessed are you who weep now, for you will laugh."},{"ref":"Luke 6:22","n":22,"text":"Blessed are you when people hate you, and when they exclude you and insult you and reject your name as evil because of the Son of Man."},{"ref":"Luke 6:23","n":23,"text":"Rejoice in that day and leap for joy, because great is your reward in heaven. For their fathers treated the prophets in the same way."},{"ref":"Luke 6:24","n":24,"text":"But woe to you who are rich, for you have already received your comfort."},{"ref":"Luke 6:25","n":25,"text":"Woe to you who are well fed now, for you will hunger. Woe to you who laugh now, for you will mourn and weep."},{"ref":"Luke 6:26","n":26,"text":"Woe to you when all men speak well of you, for their fathers treated the false prophets in the same way."},{"ref":"Luke 6:27","n":27,"text":"But to those of you who will listen, I say: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you,"},{"ref":"Luke 6:28","n":28,"text":"bless those who curse you, pray for those who mistreat you."}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Picture it, friend: Jesus has just chosen His twelve {{apostles|apostles}} up on the mountain, and now He comes down with them and stands on a level place. The crowd is HUGE. A large crowd of His {{disciple|disciples}} is there, plus a great number of people from all over {{Judea|Judea}}, Jerusalem, and the seacoast of {{Tyre|Tyre}} and {{Sidon|Sidon}}.","ref":"Luke 6:17"},{"t":"choice","q":"People came from {{Judea|Judea}}, Jerusalem, {{Tyre|Tyre}} and {{Sidon|Sidon}} to find Jesus on that level place. What did they come for?","ref":"Luke 6:17–18","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["To hear Him and to be healed","To crown Him as their king","To watch Him debate the Pharisees"],"correct":0,"expl":["That's why they made the trip. Luke puts it plainly: they \"had come to hear Him and to be healed of their diseases\" (Luke 6:18). And it doesn't stop there, friend, because \"those troubled by unclean spirits were healed\" too (Luke 6:18). Some of them came from the seacoast of Tyre and Sidon (Luke 6:17), and nobody makes a trip like that for nothing.","A coronation on a level place with no throne in sight? I love the ambition, baker. But Luke spells out why they came: they \"had come to hear Him and to be healed of their diseases\" (Luke 6:18). Open ears and aching bodies. That's what they brought.","Nobody pulled up a chair for a showdown, friend. The crowd's reason is right there: they \"had come to hear Him and to be healed of their diseases\" (Luke 6:18). People had come from all over Judea, Jerusalem, and the seacoast of Tyre and Sidon (Luke 6:17), and not for an argument. They came for His words and His healing."]},{"t":"story","text":"And now the entire crowd is pressing in, trying to touch Him. It's my bakery when the first warm batch comes out of the oven and everybody's reaching at once!","ref":"Luke 6:19"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: The crowd on the level place was trying to touch Jesus because power was coming from Him and healing them all.","ref":"Luke 6:19","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["And what a reason to push through a crowd! \"The entire crowd was trying to touch Him, because power was coming from Him and healing them all\" (Luke 6:19). All of them, baker. Not most. Not just the front row. ALL.","This one's true, friend, and it's one of my favorite lines. Power \"was coming from Him and healing them all\" (Luke 6:19), and that's why the entire crowd was reaching for Him. Remember my bakery when the warm batch comes out? Same reaching, WAY better reason."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus looks up at His {{disciple|disciples}} and starts teaching, and His first word is \"{{blessed|Blessed}}.\" But wait till you hear who He says it about.","ref":"Luke 6:20"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus looks at His {{disciple|disciples}} and starts with \"{{blessed|Blessed}}.\" Who does He say the {{kingdom of God|kingdom of God}} belongs to?","ref":"Luke 6:20","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["You who are poor","You who are powerful","You who are famous"],"correct":0,"expl":["The kingdom of God goes to the poor, friend. \"Blessed are you who are poor, for yours is the kingdom of God\" (Luke 6:20). Not a coupon. Not a gift card. The WHOLE kingdom belongs to them.","Powerful? Baker, Jesus flips that whole idea upside down. His very first blessing goes the other direction: \"Blessed are you who are poor, for yours is the kingdom of God\" (Luke 6:20). The world hands the trophy to the powerful, and Jesus opens His teaching with the poor. I spent years at the back of a junk drawer, so trust me, I like where this is going.","No red carpet in this line, friend. The blessing lands on the poor, and the kingdom of God is theirs (Luke 6:20). Nobody has to be a big name. Look at me, I lived behind the takeout menus, and NOBODY was asking for my autograph."]},{"t":"story","text":"He keeps going: \"{{blessed|Blessed}} are you who hunger now, for you will be filled.\"","ref":"Luke 6:21"},{"t":"choice","q":"Hungry now, filled later. Then Jesus says the ones who {{weep|weep}} now will ___.","ref":"Luke 6:21","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["laugh","rule","rest"],"correct":0,"expl":["Tears now, laughter later. That's the promise: \"Blessed are you who weep now, for you will laugh\" (Luke 6:21). Friend, I'd frame that line and hang it right over my oven.","Jesus picks a happier word than that. The line ends with laughter: \"Blessed are you who weep now, for you will laugh\" (Luke 6:21). No throne in this one. Just the people crying today laughing later, and honestly, baker, that sounds even better to me.","Watch how the lines pair up, friend. Hungry now, filled later. Weeping now, laughing later (Luke 6:21). I'd take a nap right this second if the ovens would let me, but the promise here is laughter."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then it gets personal. Jesus says they're {{blessed|blessed}} when people hate them, exclude them, insult them and reject their name as evil because of the {{Son of Man|Son of Man}}.","ref":"Luke 6:22"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says His {{disciple|disciples}} are {{blessed|blessed}} when people hate and insult them because of the {{Son of Man|Son of Man}}. What does He tell them to do in that day?","ref":"Luke 6:22–23","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Rejoice and leap for joy","Hide until it all passes","Shake the dust off their feet"],"correct":0,"expl":["Insulted, and jumping for joy anyway? That's the call. \"Rejoice in that day and leap for joy, because great is your reward in heaven\" (Luke 6:23). Somebody calls my stamps crooked and I sulk for a week, baker. But Jesus gives the reason, and it's a big one: a great reward in heaven.","When people insult you, crawling under the counter until it blows over sounds about right. But the instruction runs the other way: \"Rejoice in that day and leap for joy\" (Luke 6:23). Not duck. Not hide. LEAP, friend.","That phrase does show up later in Luke, baker, when Jesus sends the Twelve out to towns that won't welcome them (Luke 9:5). Here, though, the instruction is to \"leap for joy, because great is your reward in heaven\" (Luke 6:23). Hated and insulted, and the move is JUMPING for joy. Feet in the air, not dust off the feet."]},{"t":"story","text":"Why leap? Because great is their reward in heaven, and the fathers of the people insulting them treated the {{prophet|prophets}} in the same way.","ref":"Luke 6:23"},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus turns it around: {{woe|woe}} to the rich, who have already received their comfort, and woe to the well fed now, for they will hunger. That's eating the whole bowl of cookie dough before the oven's even warm.","ref":"Luke 6:24–25"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: Jesus says {{woe|woe}} to those who laugh now, because they will keep laughing forever.","ref":"Luke 6:25","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["Oh friend, that one has a twist at the end. The laughing gets a woe: \"Woe to you who laugh now, for you will mourn and weep\" (Luke 6:25). No forever laughing. It's the mirror image of the blessing back in Luke 6:21, where the ones who weep now will laugh. The laughter switches sides.","Nice catch, baker. The laughing doesn't stay. Laughing now, mourning and weeping later (Luke 6:25). It's the flip side of the earlier blessing, where the ones who weep now will laugh (Luke 6:21)."]},{"t":"story","text":"One more {{woe|woe}}. Woe to you when all men speak well of you.","ref":"Luke 6:26"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says: {{woe|Woe}} to you when all men speak well of you, for their fathers treated the ___ {{prophet|prophets}} in the same way.","ref":"Luke 6:26","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["false","old","young"],"correct":0,"expl":["Everybody clapping for you isn't always a good sign, baker. \"Woe to you when all men speak well of you, for their fathers treated the false prophets in the same way\" (Luke 6:26). A few lines back, the real prophets got the hate and the insults (Luke 6:22-23). The false ones got the applause.","It's not about how old they were, friend. The prophets in this line are the false ones. When all men speak well of you, that's how their fathers treated the false prophets (Luke 6:26). True or false is the question here, and the false ones were the crowd favorites.","Young or old doesn't matter here, baker. What matters is the praise. The woe comes \"when all men speak well of you,\" because that's how their fathers treated the false prophets (Luke 6:26). When EVERYBODY loves you, that's worth a second look."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus says, \"But to those of you who will listen, I say:\" and friend, I think this next part is the toughest stretch of the whole sermon.","ref":"Luke 6:27"},{"t":"choice","q":"To those who will listen, Jesus says: Love your ___, do good to those who hate you.","ref":"Luke 6:27","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["enemies","neighbors","leaders"],"correct":0,"expl":["Enemies. Friend, I TOLD you this was the tough stretch! First \"Love your enemies,\" and then He piles on, \"do good to those who hate you\" (Luke 6:27). Not tolerate. Not avoid. LOVE.","The neighbor who borrows your ladder? Easy to love. Jesus goes right past that to \"Love your enemies\" and \"do good to those who hate you\" (Luke 6:27). The person who can't stand you, baker? THAT'S the assignment.","Not the boss. Not the folks at the top. Jesus aims somewhere a whole lot harder, friend: the people who HATE you. Love them, and do good to them (Luke 6:27). Whew."]},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: Jesus also tells those who will listen to bless those who {{curse|curse}} them and pray for those who mistreat them.","ref":"Luke 6:28","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["Tall order, right? Bless the ones who curse you, pray for the ones who mistreat you (Luke 6:28). Friend, if somebody cursed at me, my mustache would be tied in a knot before I got one kind word out. But that's the call here: a blessing for a curse, and a prayer for mistreatment.","Oh, it's in there, baker. Right after loving your enemies (Luke 6:27), Jesus says, \"bless those who curse you, pray for those who mistreat you\" (Luke 6:28). He just keeps turning up the oven."]}]},{"id":"s2","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 2","range":"Luke 6:29–38","verses":[{"ref":"Luke 6:29","n":29,"text":"If someone strikes you on one cheek, turn to him the other also. And if someone takes your cloak, do not withhold your tunic as well."},{"ref":"Luke 6:30","n":30,"text":"Give to everyone who asks you, and if anyone takes what is yours, do not demand it back."},{"ref":"Luke 6:31","n":31,"text":"Do to others as you would have them do to you."},{"ref":"Luke 6:32","n":32,"text":"If you love those who love you, what credit is that to you? Even sinners love those who love them."},{"ref":"Luke 6:33","n":33,"text":"If you do good to those who do good to you, what credit is that to you? Even sinners do the same."},{"ref":"Luke 6:34","n":34,"text":"And if you lend to those from whom you expect repayment, what credit is that to you? Even sinners lend to sinners, expecting to be repaid in full."},{"ref":"Luke 6:35","n":35,"text":"But love your enemies, do good to them, and lend to them, expecting nothing in return. Then your reward will be great, and you will be sons of the Most High; for He is kind to the ungrateful and wicked."},{"ref":"Luke 6:36","n":36,"text":"Be merciful, just as your Father is merciful."},{"ref":"Luke 6:37","n":37,"text":"Do not judge, and you will not be judged. Do not condemn, and you will not be condemned. Forgive, and you will be forgiven."},{"ref":"Luke 6:38","n":38,"text":"Give, and it will be given to you. A good measure, pressed down, shaken together, and running over will be poured into your lap. For with the measure you use, it will be measured back to you.”"}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Jesus keeps the hard sayings coming: \"If someone strikes you on one cheek, turn to him the other also.\"","ref":"Luke 6:29"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says that if someone takes your {{cloak|cloak}}, there's something you should not hold back. What is it?","ref":"Luke 6:29","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Your tunic as well","Your sandals as well","Your belt as well"],"correct":0,"expl":["Cloak AND tunic! \"And if someone takes your cloak, do not withhold your tunic as well\" (Luke 6:29). Somebody grabs your coat and you hand over the shirt too? That's generosity with NO brakes, baker.","The verse never gets down to the feet, friend. It stays with the clothes on your back. Somebody takes the cloak, and you don't hold back the tunic (Luke 6:29). That's both layers walking out the door.","I get nervous lending out my good apron, baker, and Jesus goes WAY past a belt. The verse names the cloak and then says, \"do not withhold your tunic as well\" (Luke 6:29). The shirt off your back!"]},{"t":"story","text":"Give to everyone who asks you, Jesus says, and if anyone takes what is yours, do not demand it back. WHEW, that's somebody walking off with your phone charger and you NEVER sending the \"hey, you still got my charger?\" text.","ref":"Luke 6:30"},{"t":"choice","q":"Then Jesus gives a rule for how to treat people. What is it?","ref":"Luke 6:31","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Do to others as you would have them do to you","Treat others the way they have treated you","Do good to others when they have earned it"],"correct":0,"expl":["Short, simple, and it would fit on the lid of a cookie box: \"Do to others as you would have them do to you\" (Luke 6:31). Want kindness, friend? Hand it out first.","That's the rule most of us actually live by, baker, and I'm guilty too. But this rule points forward, not back: \"Do to others as you would have them do to you\" (Luke 6:31). Not how they DID treat you. How you'd WANT to be treated. Big difference, especially right after He said to love your enemies (Luke 6:27).","Earned? Jesus tosses the scorecard right out the window, friend. The rule is \"Do to others as you would have them do to you\" (Luke 6:31), and nobody has to earn it first. You start with how you'd want to be treated, and you go."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus asks a question: \"If you love those who love you, what {{credit|credit}} is that to you?\"","ref":"Luke 6:32"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: Jesus says even sinners love those who love them.","ref":"Luke 6:32","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["Loving the people who love you back is the easy batch, friend. ANYBODY can bake that one. Jesus puts it this way: \"Even sinners love those who love them\" (Luke 6:32).","There's a question first, baker: \"If you love those who love you, what credit is that to you?\" And then the answer: \"Even sinners love those who love them\" (Luke 6:32). Love that only goes to people who love you back? Sinners have that one covered. Jesus is asking for a lot more."]},{"t":"story","text":"Same goes for doing good only to those who do good to you, and for lending only when you expect to be paid back. Even sinners lend to sinners, expecting to be repaid in full.","ref":"Luke 6:33–34"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: Jesus tells them to lend, expecting nothing in return.","ref":"Luke 6:35","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["NOTHING in return! Love your enemies, do good to them, and lend to them, \"expecting nothing in return\" (Luke 6:35). No IOU, no reminder text, no awkward hint. Remember the charger walking off and you never sending the \"hey, you still got my charger?\" text? Like that, baker, but this time you're the one handing it over.","Sounds wild, I know, friend. Sinners lend expecting to be repaid in full (Luke 6:34). Then Jesus says, \"But love your enemies, do good to them, and lend to them, expecting nothing in return\" (Luke 6:35). Lend anyway, and don't hold your breath waiting for it to come back."]},{"t":"story","text":"And then the payoff: their reward will be great, and they will be sons of the {{Most High|Most High}}. Friend, that's a better title than anything the Millers ever carved on MY handle.","ref":"Luke 6:35"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says the {{Most High|Most High}} is kind to the ___ and wicked.","ref":"Luke 6:35","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["ungrateful","humble","faithful"],"correct":0,"expl":["People who never say thank you, and the Most High is STILL kind to them. He \"is kind to the ungrateful and wicked\" (Luke 6:35). Friend, if somebody skipped the thank you on one cookie, my mustache would droop for the rest of the day.","Here's where it gets surprising, friend. Jesus tells the ones who love their enemies that they \"will be sons of the Most High; for He is kind to the ungrateful and wicked\" (Luke 6:35). The UNGRATEFUL, baker. Not exactly the crowd you'd expect on that list.","Kind to the faithful? Nobody would blink at that, baker. The shocker is who actually gets named: the Most High \"is kind to the ungrateful and wicked\" (Luke 6:35). The ungrateful AND the wicked! That's kindness going to people who did nothing to earn it."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus says: \"Be {{merciful|merciful}}, just as your Father is merciful.\"","ref":"Luke 6:36"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says: Do not judge, and you will not be ___. Do not {{condemn|condemn}}, and you will not be condemned. Forgive, and you will be ___.","ref":"Luke 6:37","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["judged","forgiven","praised","honored","remembered"],"blanks":2,"correctSet":[0,1],"correct":0,"expl":["First blank, nailed. \"Do not judge, and you will not be judged\" (Luke 6:37). Judgment works like a boomerang, friend. Don't throw it, and it doesn't come flying back at you.","I don't hold grudges, baker, and this line is a big reason why. \"Forgive, and you will be forgiven\" (Luke 6:37). That's your second blank. You let go, and you get let go.","See the pattern, friend? \"Do not judge, and you will not be judged.\" Then, \"Forgive, and you will be forgiven\" (Luke 6:37). The word that goes out is the word that comes back, so praised doesn't fit either blank.","Jesus pairs each action with its own echo, baker. Don't judge, and you won't be judged. \"Forgive, and you will be forgiven\" (Luke 6:37). It's a matching set, like a stamp and the print it leaves, and honored doesn't match either one.","I like where your heart's at, friend, but Jesus keeps the words matched up. Don't judge, and you won't be judged. Forgive, and \"you will be forgiven\" (Luke 6:37). Whatever you send out, that same word comes right back."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus says, \"Give, and it will be given to you.\" And He paints a picture of how it comes back to you.","ref":"Luke 6:38"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says that when you give, it will be poured into your {{lap|lap}}. What does He say gets poured in?","ref":"Luke 6:38","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["A good measure pressed down and running over","A small portion carefully counted out","A little left over from the harvest"],"correct":0,"expl":["I pack my cookie boxes with my mustache, baker, and even I can't get them THAT full. The picture is \"A good measure, pressed down, shaken together, and running over\" poured into your lap (Luke 6:38). That's generosity spilling over the top.","Carefully counted? Friend, Jesus is not stingy with this picture. Pressed down so more fits, shaken so it settles, and STILL running over, all of it \"poured into your lap\" (Luke 6:38).","Jesus goes way bigger than leftovers, baker. He describes \"A good measure, pressed down, shaken together, and running over\" poured into your lap (Luke 6:38). That's not the crumbs at the bottom of the cookie box. That's a box so full it's landing in your lap."]},{"t":"choice","q":"Then Jesus finishes this part: For with the ___ you use, it will be {{measure|measured}} back to you.","ref":"Luke 6:38","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["measure","mercy","money"],"correct":0,"expl":["Big scoop out, big scoop back, friend. \"For with the measure you use, it will be measured back to you\" (Luke 6:38). Use a stingy little teaspoon on others, and, well, you can see where this goes.","Mercy did just come up, baker, when Jesus said, \"Be merciful, just as your Father is merciful\" (Luke 6:36). But this blank is \"measure,\" as in \"the measure you use\" (Luke 6:38). Whatever cup you pour with is the cup that gets poured back.","This verse isn't counting coins, friend. It's about the measuring itself: \"with the measure you use, it will be measured back to you\" (Luke 6:38). The way you measure for others is the way it gets measured for you."]}]},{"id":"s3","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 3","range":"Luke 6:39–49","verses":[{"ref":"Luke 6:39","n":39,"text":"Jesus also told them a parable: “Can a blind man lead a blind man? Will they not both fall into a pit?"},{"ref":"Luke 6:40","n":40,"text":"A disciple is not above his teacher, but everyone who is fully trained will be like his teacher."},{"ref":"Luke 6:41","n":41,"text":"Why do you look at the speck in your brother’s eye but fail to notice the beam in your own eye?"},{"ref":"Luke 6:42","n":42,"text":"How can you say, ‘Brother, let me take the speck out of your eye,’ while you yourself fail to see the beam in your own eye? You hypocrite! First take the beam out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother’s eye."},{"ref":"Luke 6:43","n":43,"text":"No good tree bears bad fruit, nor does a bad tree bear good fruit."},{"ref":"Luke 6:44","n":44,"text":"For each tree is known by its own fruit. Indeed, figs are not gathered from thornbushes, nor grapes from brambles."},{"ref":"Luke 6:45","n":45,"text":"The good man brings good things out of the good treasure of his heart, and the evil man brings evil things out of the evil treasure of his heart. For out of the overflow of the heart the mouth speaks."},{"ref":"Luke 6:46","n":46,"text":"Why do you call Me ‘Lord, Lord,’ but do not do what I say?"},{"ref":"Luke 6:47","n":47,"text":"I will show you what he is like who comes to Me and hears My words and acts on them:"},{"ref":"Luke 6:48","n":48,"text":"He is like a man building a house, who dug down deep and laid his foundation on the rock. When the flood came, the torrent crashed against that house but could not shake it, because it was well built."},{"ref":"Luke 6:49","n":49,"text":"But the one who hears My words and does not act on them is like a man who built his house on ground without a foundation. The torrent crashed against that house, and immediately it fell — and great was its destruction!”"}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Jesus also tells them a {{parable|parable}}, and it starts with a question: \"Can a blind man lead a blind man?\"","ref":"Luke 6:39"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus asks if a blind man can lead a blind man. Where does He say they will both end up?","ref":"Luke 6:39","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["In a pit","In the Jordan","In the wilderness"],"correct":0,"expl":["Two guys who can't see, one leading the other. Where else would that end? \"Will they not both fall into a pit?\" (Luke 6:39). I'm picturing the leader going, \"Trust me, I know a shortcut,\" right before they BOTH go down.","No river in this parable, friend. The question ends in a pit (Luke 6:39), and the leader and the follower both go in together. Honestly, a splash in the Jordan would've been the nicer ending.","Wandering around lost would be bad enough, baker. This ends worse: \"Will they not both fall into a pit?\" (Luke 6:39). One blind man leading another, and they both go right in."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus says a {{disciple|disciple}} is not above his teacher.","ref":"Luke 6:40"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: Jesus says everyone who is fully trained will be like his teacher.","ref":"Luke 6:40","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["Fully trained means looking like the one who trained you, friend. A disciple is not above his teacher, \"but everyone who is fully trained will be like his teacher\" (Luke 6:40). My early stamps looked like a smudge, so I'm VERY glad nobody was training under me back then.","Not above the teacher, but like him. That's the line, baker: \"everyone who is fully trained will be like his teacher\" (Luke 6:40). The finished student ends up looking like the one who did the training."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus asks why you look at the {{speck|speck}} in your brother's eye. A speck, so tiny you need glasses just to find it.","ref":"Luke 6:41"},{"t":"choice","q":"You spot the {{speck|speck}} in your brother's eye. What does Jesus say you fail to notice in your own eye?","ref":"Luke 6:41","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["A beam","A pebble","A thorn"],"correct":0,"expl":["Remember needing glasses just to find that speck? The beam, you could find with your eyes closed, friend. Jesus asks why you look at the speck in your brother's eye but fail to notice \"the beam in your own eye\" (Luke 6:41).","This picture goes WAY bigger than a pebble, baker: \"the beam in your own eye\" (Luke 6:41). A beam, a big piece of timber. Tiny thing over there, giant thing right here.","Speck versus BEAM, friend. On one side, the speck in your brother's eye. On the other, \"the beam in your own eye\" (Luke 6:41). One you squint to see, the other you could build a porch with. A thorn doesn't even come close."]},{"t":"story","text":"Jesus pictures that person offering to take the {{speck|speck}} out of his brother's eye while missing the {{beam|beam}} in his own. I can see it now: a whole BEAM sticking out of his face, and he's leaning in, \"Hold still, buddy, you got a little something.\"","ref":"Luke 6:42"},{"t":"choice","q":"What does Jesus call the person who offers to fix his brother's {{speck|speck}} but can't see the {{beam|beam}} in his own eye?","ref":"Luke 6:42","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["You hypocrite","You fool","You brood of vipers"],"correct":0,"expl":["Jesus says it straight: \"You hypocrite!\" (Luke 6:42). The guy's offering to fix his brother's speck with a whole beam sticking out of his own eye. \"Hold still, buddy,\" indeed.","Jesus picks a sharper word, baker: \"You hypocrite!\" (Luke 6:42). The problem is acting like the speck expert while there's a beam in your own eye. Spotting the speck in your brother's eye and missing the beam in your own.","Those words come from John the Baptist, friend, when he spoke to the crowds coming out to be baptized (Luke 3:7). Here the word is \"You hypocrite!\" (Luke 6:42). It fits the picture perfectly. Somebody acting like he can see well enough to help, with a beam in his own eye."]},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: Jesus says to first take the {{beam|beam}} out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the {{speck|speck}} from your brother's eye.","ref":"Luke 6:42","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["Beam FIRST, friend. \"First take the beam out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother’s eye\" (Luke 6:42). Notice the speck doesn't get forgotten. It just waits its turn. Clean your own counter, then go help with your neighbor's.","The order is the whole point, baker. Beam out of your own eye first, and then you see clearly enough to remove the speck from your brother's eye (Luke 6:42). Do it the other way around and you're just poking at your brother with a beam on your face."]},{"t":"story","text":"Now the picture changes to trees. Jesus says no good tree bears bad fruit, nor does a bad tree bear good fruit.","ref":"Luke 6:43"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says each tree is known by its own ___.","ref":"Luke 6:44","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["fruit","roots","leaves"],"correct":0,"expl":["You don't need a name tag on a fig tree, friend. Just look at what's hanging off the branches. \"For each tree is known by its own fruit\" (Luke 6:44).","Roots are hiding underground, baker, and you can't see them from the road. It's the fruit that gives a tree away (Luke 6:44). What a tree produces tells you what it is. Same way a bakery gets known by its cookies, not its oven.","Lots of trees have leaves, friend, so leaves won't tell you much. Figs don't come from thornbushes, and grapes don't come from brambles. Each tree \"is known by its own fruit\" (Luke 6:44). What grows on the branches tells the story."]},{"t":"story","text":"{{figs|Figs}} are not gathered from thornbushes, Jesus says, nor grapes from {{brambles|brambles}}, and friend, if I go looking for chocolate chips in the junk drawer, all I'm coming back with is rubber bands. Then Jesus says the good man brings good things out of the good treasure of his heart, and the evil man brings evil things out of the evil treasure of his heart.","ref":"Luke 6:44–45"},{"t":"choice","q":"The good man brings good things out of the good treasure of his heart. The evil man brings evil things out of the evil treasure of his. Then Jesus says: Out of the overflow of the heart the ___ speaks.","ref":"Luke 6:45","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["mouth","hand","eye"],"correct":0,"expl":["Whatever's stored up in the heart comes out when you talk, baker. \"For out of the overflow of the heart the mouth speaks\" (Luke 6:45). It's like a cookie jar. Tip it over and you find out real quick what was inside.","This verse is about talking, friend, not doing: out of the overflow of the heart, the mouth speaks (Luke 6:45). What fills the heart spills out in words. Your talk shows what's been stored up inside. My mustache can point and wave all day, but it's my mouth that gives me away.","The eye already had its big moment with that beam, baker! This time it's the mouth. The good treasure or the evil treasure of the heart comes out in what we say (Luke 6:45). Listen to somebody talk long enough and you'll smell what's baking."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus asks His listeners a sharp question: \"Why do you call Me ‘Lord, Lord,’ but do not do what I say?\"","ref":"Luke 6:46"},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus says He will show them what someone is like who comes to Him, hears His words and acts on them. He is like a man building a house, who dug down deep and laid his {{foundation|foundation}} on the rock.","ref":"Luke 6:47–48"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: When the flood came and the {{torrent|torrent}} crashed against the house built on the rock, the house was shaken but still stood.","ref":"Luke 6:48","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["This house didn't even get shaken, friend. The torrent \"crashed against that house but could not shake it, because it was well built\" (Luke 6:48). The flood came, the water hit, and NOTHING. That's what digging deep and building on the rock gets you.","You caught it, baker. Not shaken. Not even a wobble. The torrent \"could not shake it, because it was well built\" (Luke 6:48). Dug down deep, foundation on the rock, and the flood got nowhere."]},{"t":"story","text":"But the one who hears His words and does not act on them, Jesus says, is like a man who built his house on ground without a {{foundation|foundation}}.","ref":"Luke 6:49"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says the one who hears His words and does not act on them is like a man who built his house on ground without a {{foundation|foundation}}. What happens when the {{torrent|torrent}} crashes against that house?","ref":"Luke 6:49","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["It fell right away and was destroyed","It leaned hard but stayed standing","It was damaged and rebuilt later"],"correct":0,"expl":["No foundation, no chance, baker. \"The torrent crashed against that house, and immediately it fell\" and \"great was its destruction!\" (Luke 6:49). That's how Jesus ends this whole teaching, with the difference between hearing His words and acting on them.","That house never even got the chance to lean, friend. It fell \"immediately\" when the torrent crashed against it, \"and great was its destruction!\" (Luke 6:49). IMMEDIATELY. No foundation, no hanging on, just down.","Jesus never mentions a rebuild, baker. The house fell immediately, and \"great was its destruction!\" (Luke 6:49). Down fast and wrecked. That's the picture Jesus paints of someone who hears His words and doesn't act on them."]}]},{"id":"b","kind":"burnt","title":"Burnt Cookies","intro":"One last retelling, friend. Two of these cookies stayed in my oven too long, so each one has a detail that's wrong. Tap the 2 burnt ones.","lines":[{"text":"Jesus comes down and stands on a level place, and the crowd is HUGE: people from all over Judea, Jerusalem, and the seacoast of Tyre and Sidon. They came to hear Him and to be healed, and everybody's trying to touch Him, because power is coming from Him and healing them all.","ref":"Luke 6:17–19","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Jesus looks up at His disciples: blessed are you who are poor, you who hunger now, you who weep now. Then He flips the whole tray over: woe to you who are rich, you who are well fed now, you who laugh now, and woe when all men speak well of you.","ref":"Luke 6:20–26","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Now the TOUGH part: love your enemies, turn the other cheek, give to everyone who asks, and lend expecting nothing in return. Do that, and your reward will be great, and you'll be sons of Abraham. So be merciful, just as your Father is merciful. Friend, I'm still working on the cheek one.","ref":"Luke 6:27–36","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"sons of Abraham","true":"sons of the Most High","ref":"Luke 6:35"}]},{"text":"Don't judge, don't condemn, forgive. Give, and a good measure, pressed down and running over, gets poured into your lap. And before you go after the speck in your brother's eye, get the BEAM out of your own. Each tree is known by its own fruit, and out of the overflow of the heart the mouth speaks.","ref":"Luke 6:37–45","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Why call Him Lord, Lord and not do what He says? The one who hears and acts is like a man who dug down deep and built on the rock, and the flood couldn't shake it. The one who hears and doesn't act built his house on the sand, and when the torrent hit, it fell immediately. Great was its destruction.","ref":"Luke 6:46–49","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"built his house on the sand","true":"built his house on ground without a foundation","ref":"Luke 6:49"}]}]},{"id":"e","kind":"ending","title":"The Big Idea","lesson":"","bigIdea":["Okay friend, here's the whole sermon in one box. Jesus flips what counts as blessed. He calls the poor, the hungry and the weeping blessed, and says \"woe\" to the rich, the well fed and the ones laughing now (Luke 6:20-26). What you've got NOW isn't the whole story, because \"great is your reward in heaven\" (Luke 6:23).","And the heart of it is the hardest thing in the recipe: love your enemies (Luke 6:27), give without keeping score (Luke 6:30), lend expecting nothing in return (Luke 6:35). Why? Because that's what God is like. \"He is kind to the ungrateful and wicked\" (Luke 6:35).","No scoreboard. No receipts. No \"remember when I helped you move?\" Just \"Be merciful, just as your Father is merciful\" (Luke 6:36). And the measure you use is the measure that comes back to you (Luke 6:38).","And before you go correcting anybody, check who's leading you and check your own eye. A student ends up like his teacher (Luke 6:40), and a blind guide just walks you both into a pit (Luke 6:39). So deal with your own BEAM first. Then you \"will see clearly\" enough to help your brother with his speck (Luke 6:42). Trust me, friend. I stamped crooked for YEARS. I am the LAST stamper who should be inspecting other people's cookies.","Here's what's underneath all of it: what comes out of you shows what's inside you. A tree is known by its fruit, and \"out of the overflow of the heart the mouth speaks\" (Luke 6:44-45). So it's not about looking good on the outside. It's about what's stored up in the heart, because that's what comes out (Luke 6:45).","So here's the big idea: hearing isn't enough, and calling Him \"Lord, Lord\" isn't enough. The one who hears His words and acts on them builds on the rock, and when the torrent hits, that house doesn't shake (Luke 6:46-49).","Reading the recipe and baking the recipe are two VERY different things. Ask anybody who's read the instructions twice and never once turned the oven on. Zero cookies, friend."]}],"words":{"apostles":{"word":"apostles","meaning":"The twelve men Jesus picked out from His followers and sent out to speak for Him and carry His message.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"disciple":{"word":"disciple","meaning":"A follower who learns from a teacher. Here it means the people who followed Jesus to learn from Him.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Judea":{"word":"Judea","meaning":"The southern part of the land of Israel, where the city of Jerusalem was.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Tyre":{"word":"Tyre","meaning":"An old port city on the Mediterranean coast, north of Israel, famous for its trade by sea. Its people were mostly not Jewish.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Sidon":{"word":"Sidon","meaning":"A very old port city on the Mediterranean coast, a little north of Tyre. Its people were mostly not Jewish.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Pharisees":{"word":"Pharisees","meaning":"A Jewish religious group known for keeping God's law and their own added rules very strictly. They often argued with Jesus.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"blessed":{"word":"blessed","meaning":"Truly happy and well off because God shows them His favor.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"kingdom of God":{"word":"kingdom of God","meaning":"God's rule as King, and the place and people where He reigns.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"weep":{"word":"weep","meaning":"To cry, with tears, because of sadness.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Son of Man":{"word":"Son of Man","meaning":"A title Jesus often used for Himself. It points to Him as a real human and also as the one God promised to send.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"rejoice":{"word":"rejoice","meaning":"To be very glad and show your joy.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"prophet":{"word":"prophet","meaning":"A person who speaks messages from God to people. A false prophet claims to speak for God but does not.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"woe":{"word":"woe","meaning":"Great sorrow or trouble. Saying \"woe to you\" is a warning that hard trouble is coming.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"curse":{"word":"curse","meaning":"To speak words wishing harm or evil on someone.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"cloak":{"word":"cloak","meaning":"A loose outer coat worn over other clothes, also used as a blanket at night.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"tunic":{"word":"tunic","meaning":"A long shirt-like garment worn under the cloak, next to the body.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary","Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"credit":{"word":"credit","meaning":"Praise or thanks you earn for what you do.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Most High":{"word":"Most High","meaning":"A name for God, the one who is above all.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"merciful":{"word":"merciful","meaning":"Kind and forgiving to others, even when they deserve punishment.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"condemn":{"word":"condemn","meaning":"To declare someone guilty and deserving of punishment.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"measure":{"word":"measure","meaning":"An amount of something, like grain, scooped out with a set-size container. It can also mean the container used to measure.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"lap":{"word":"lap","meaning":"The loose front fold of a robe, which people could hold out like a bag to carry grain.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"parable":{"word":"parable","meaning":"A short story or word picture from everyday life used to teach a lesson about God.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Jordan":{"word":"Jordan","meaning":"The main river of the land of Israel. It runs south from the Sea of Galilee to the Dead Sea.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"wilderness":{"word":"wilderness","meaning":"Wild, open land with few people, often dry and used only for grazing animals.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"speck":{"word":"speck","meaning":"A tiny bit of something, like a crumb of dust or a splinter of wood.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"beam":{"word":"beam","meaning":"A big, long piece of wood used to hold up a roof or building.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"hypocrite":{"word":"hypocrite","meaning":"Someone who pretends to be good or better than they really are, like an actor playing a part.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"brood of vipers":{"word":"brood of vipers","meaning":"A nest of baby poisonous snakes. Used as an insult for people who are sneaky and harmful.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary","Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"figs":{"word":"figs","meaning":"Sweet, soft fruits that grow on fig trees, a common food in Bible lands.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"brambles":{"word":"brambles","meaning":"Prickly, thorny bushes that tangle and scratch.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"foundation":{"word":"foundation","meaning":"The solid bottom part a building rests on, which holds it steady.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"torrent":{"word":"torrent","meaning":"A fast, powerful rush of water, like a flooding stream.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"idiom:863":{"word":"poured into your lap","meaning":"To be given a generous return, handed right to you. The \"lap\" is the large fold of a robe over the chest, which people used as a pocket. The measure is pictured as grain pressed down and overflowing.","sources":["Ellicott","Barnes","Gill","Cambridge","Pulpit"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"}}},{"id":"r5-luke16","type":"Parables","title":"The Parable of the Shrewd Manager","ref":"Luke 16:1–13","round":5,"parts":[{"id":"s1","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 1","range":"Luke 16:1–8","verses":[{"ref":"Luke 16:1","n":1,"text":"Jesus also said to His disciples, “There was a rich man whose manager was accused of wasting his possessions."},{"ref":"Luke 16:2","n":2,"text":"So he called him in to ask, ‘What is this I hear about you? Turn in an account of your management, for you cannot be manager any longer.’"},{"ref":"Luke 16:3","n":3,"text":"The manager said to himself, ‘What shall I do, now that my master is taking away my position? I am too weak to dig and too ashamed to beg."},{"ref":"Luke 16:4","n":4,"text":"I know what I will do so that after my removal from management, people will welcome me into their homes.’"},{"ref":"Luke 16:5","n":5,"text":"And he called in each one of his master’s debtors. ‘How much do you owe my master?’ he asked the first."},{"ref":"Luke 16:6","n":6,"text":"‘A hundred measures of olive oil,’ he answered. ‘Take your bill,’ said the manager, ‘sit down quickly, and write fifty.’"},{"ref":"Luke 16:7","n":7,"text":"Then he asked another, ‘And how much do you owe?’ ‘A hundred measures of wheat,’ he replied. ‘Take your bill and write eighty,’ he told him."},{"ref":"Luke 16:8","n":8,"text":"The master commended the dishonest manager because he had acted shrewdly. For the sons of this age are more shrewd in dealing with their own kind than are the sons of light."}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Jesus tells His {{disciples|disciples}} a story: \"There was a rich man whose manager was {{accused|accused}} of wasting his {{possessions|possessions}}.\" That's like handing a friend your cookie box to hold and getting it back full of crumbs. So the rich man calls the manager in to ask, \"What is this I hear about you?\"","ref":"Luke 16:1–2"},{"t":"choice","q":"The rich man calls his manager in and asks what this is he's hearing about him. Then what does he tell the manager?","ref":"Luke 16:2","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Turn in your account since you can't stay manager","Pay back every coin by tomorrow","Work for free until it's fixed"],"correct":0,"expl":["The rich man skips the warning and goes straight to the verdict: \"Turn in an account of your management, for you cannot be manager any longer.\" (Luke 16:2) That's not a talk, friend. That's the apron coming off. Hand in the books, and the job is over.","No deadline, no coins, no repayment plan. The rich man tells him, \"Turn in an account of your management, for you cannot be manager any longer.\" (Luke 16:2) He wants the books, and the job is done. Honestly, if somebody told me to pay back every cookie I ever borrowed by TOMORROW, I'd be stamping all night.","Work for free? Friend, nobody in this story gets a second shift. The rich man says, \"Turn in an account of your management, for you cannot be manager any longer.\" (Luke 16:2) Hand in the account, and that's the end of it. No unpaid overtime to bake his way back in."]},{"t":"story","text":"The manager said to himself, \"What shall I do, now that my master is taking away my {{position|position}}?\" He runs down his other options for work, and I bet he likes them about as much as I liked the back of the drawer behind the takeout menus.","ref":"Luke 16:3"},{"t":"choice","q":"The manager tells himself he can always fall back on digging or begging once this job is gone.","ref":"Luke 16:3","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["He says the exact opposite! Listen to him: \"I am too weak to dig and too ashamed to beg.\" (Luke 16:3) Digging's out. Begging's out. Both backup plans hit the floor before he finishes the sentence, like me dropping a whole tray on the way to the oven.","You caught it. The manager crosses off both options himself: \"I am too weak to dig and too ashamed to beg.\" (Luke 16:3) That's a man scrolling job listings going nope. Nope. NOPE. With both doors shut, he's going to need a brand new plan."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then it clicks. He knows what he will do, and just like that he's got a plan for life after this job.","ref":"Luke 16:4"},{"t":"choice","q":"The manager's plan is for people to welcome him into their ___ after his removal from management.","ref":"Luke 16:4","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["homes","palaces","tents"],"correct":0,"expl":["Homes it is! The manager says, \"I know what I will do so that after my removal from management, people will welcome me into their homes.\" (Luke 16:4) The job's going away, so he's thinking about who'll still open the door for him. He's the coworker passing out cookies his last week so everybody keeps inviting him over.","Palaces? Friend, this man is aiming way lower than that. His plan is that \"people will welcome me into their homes.\" (Luke 16:4) Regular homes. He's not dreaming about a throne. He just hopes somebody still answers when he knocks.","No tents in this plan, baker. The manager says \"people will welcome me into their homes.\" (Luke 16:4) Homes! He's not planning a camping trip after he loses the job. He wants a couch and a door that opens."]},{"t":"story","text":"So he calls in each one of his master's {{debtors|debtors}}, everybody who owes the boss. He starts with the first one: \"How much do you owe my master?\"","ref":"Luke 16:5"},{"t":"choice","q":"The manager asks the first debtor how much he owes the master. What does the debtor say?","ref":"Luke 16:6","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["A hundred measures of olive oil","A hundred measures of barley","A hundred jars of honey"],"correct":0,"expl":["Olive oil, a hundred measures of it! \"A hundred measures of olive oil,\" he answered. (Luke 16:6) That's no little tab at the corner store, friend. My biggest tab ever was three cookies and an apology.","Barley's a grain, and it's not on this man's bill. \"A hundred measures of olive oil,\" he answered. (Luke 16:6) Grain does show up on the next bill, but it's a hundred measures of wheat, not barley (Luke 16:7). This first man owes oil, and plenty of it.","No honey on this bill, friend. \"A hundred measures of olive oil,\" he answered. (Luke 16:6) Not a jar in sight. Just oil, and a whole lot of it sitting on one man's tab."]},{"t":"story","text":"The manager tells him to take his {{bill|bill}}, sit down QUICKLY and write a new number. Friend, I have never seen paperwork move that fast in my life.","ref":"Luke 16:6"},{"t":"choice","q":"The first debtor owes a hundred {{measures|measures}} of olive oil. The manager tells him to take his {{bill|bill}}, sit down quickly and write ___.","ref":"Luke 16:6","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["fifty","two hundred","three hundred"],"correct":0,"expl":["FIFTY! The manager says, \"Take your bill,\" and then, \"sit down quickly, and write fifty.\" (Luke 16:6) A hundred measures of olive oil cut in half, faster than my oven timer can beep. Imagine opening a bill and the total is HALF of what it said yesterday. I'd frame that bill, friend.","Two hundred would double the bill, and the manager goes the other way! He tells the debtor to \"sit down quickly, and write fifty.\" (Luke 16:6) A hundred down to fifty. If somebody offered to chop my tab in half, I'd sit down quickly too.","Three hundred? Friend, that debtor would be up and out the door! The manager says, \"sit down quickly, and write fifty.\" (Luke 16:6) The number goes DOWN, from a hundred to fifty. Half the bill, gone, just like that."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then the manager asks another debtor, \"And how much do you owe?\" The man says he owes a hundred {{measures|measures}} of wheat.","ref":"Luke 16:7"},{"t":"choice","q":"The second debtor owes a hundred {{measures|measures}} of wheat. The manager tells him to take his {{bill|bill}} and write eighty.","ref":"Luke 16:7","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["True! \"Take your bill and write eighty,\" he told him. (Luke 16:7) A hundred measures of wheat down to eighty. The olive oil man got cut to fifty (Luke 16:6), so this one's a smaller cut, but I bet nobody owing that wheat was complaining.","This one's actually true, friend. The second debtor owes a hundred measures of wheat, and then: \"Take your bill and write eighty,\" he told him. (Luke 16:7) The oil went to fifty (Luke 16:6), the wheat went to eighty. Different numbers, and both bills got smaller."]},{"t":"story","text":"Now the master hears what his manager has done. Friend, I'm holding my breath like I'm watching a batch through the oven window.","ref":"Luke 16:8"},{"t":"choice","q":"The master hears what the dishonest manager did with the {{debts|debts}}. What does the master do?","ref":"Luke 16:8","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["He commends him for acting shrewdly","He has him thrown into prison","He makes him pay back the difference"],"correct":0,"expl":["He commends him! \"The master commended the dishonest manager because he had acted shrewdly.\" (Luke 16:8) I was watching through the oven window waiting for smoke, and out comes a compliment. Look close at what gets praised, though. It's the shrewdness, and the verse still calls him the dishonest manager.","The text never mentions a prison, baker. Here's what it says: \"The master commended the dishonest manager because he had acted shrewdly.\" (Luke 16:8) I was bracing for a cell door too, and instead he gets praised for being shrewd. My eyebrows went so high they nearly knocked my hat off.","You'd think so, right? But the text never mentions him paying anything back. \"The master commended the dishonest manager because he had acted shrewdly.\" (Luke 16:8) Commended, friend! The master praises how shrewd he was, and the same verse keeps calling him dishonest."]},{"t":"story","text":"Remember, the one who just got {{commend|commended}} for acting {{shrewd|shrewdly}} is the DISHONEST manager. Then comes a line that sizes up two groups side by side: the {{sons of this age|sons of this age}} and the {{sons of light|sons of light}}.","ref":"Luke 16:8"},{"t":"choice","q":"The line says the {{sons of light|sons of light}} are more {{shrewd|shrewd}} in dealing with their own kind than the {{sons of this age|sons of this age}}.","ref":"Luke 16:8","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["It's flipped, friend. The line says \"the sons of this age are more shrewd in dealing with their own kind than are the sons of light.\" (Luke 16:8) The sons of this age come out ahead on shrewdness here. Easy to swap them, since they're sitting right next to each other in one sentence.","Right, it's the other way around! \"For the sons of this age are more shrewd in dealing with their own kind than are the sons of light.\" (Luke 16:8) The sons of this age win the shrewdness contest. Kind of stings for the sons of light, doesn't it? I felt that one right through my apron."]}]},{"id":"s2","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 2","range":"Luke 16:9–13","verses":[{"ref":"Luke 16:9","n":9,"text":"I tell you, use worldly wealth to make friends for yourselves so that when it is gone, they will welcome you into eternal dwellings."},{"ref":"Luke 16:10","n":10,"text":"Whoever is faithful with very little will also be faithful with much, and whoever is dishonest with very little will also be dishonest with much."},{"ref":"Luke 16:11","n":11,"text":"So if you have not been faithful with worldly wealth, who will entrust you with true riches?"},{"ref":"Luke 16:12","n":12,"text":"And if you have not been faithful with the belongings of another, who will give you belongings of your own?"},{"ref":"Luke 16:13","n":13,"text":"No servant can serve two masters. Either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and money.”"}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus says, \"I tell you, use {{worldly wealth|worldly wealth}} to make friends for yourselves\" and He doesn't pretend the money sticks around. Money's like a fresh cookie on my counter, baker, and it does NOT last.","ref":"Luke 16:9"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says to use {{worldly wealth|worldly wealth}} to make friends. When that wealth is gone, what will those friends welcome you into?","ref":"Luke 16:9","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Eternal dwellings","The courts of kings","Houses of ivory"],"correct":0,"expl":["Eternal dwellings! Jesus says, \"use worldly wealth to make friends for yourselves so that when it is gone, they will welcome you into eternal dwellings.\" (Luke 16:9) Remember that fresh cookie on my counter? The money goes just like that. But notice the word welcome, the same word the manager used about homes (Luke 16:4), and this time the dwellings are eternal.","No royal courts here, friend. Jesus says \"they will welcome you into eternal dwellings.\" (Luke 16:9) The manager was hoping for homes back in his plan (Luke 16:4). Jesus talks about dwellings that are eternal, and that beats any guest list a king could make.","Houses of ivory sound shiny, like that fancy new stamper the Millers brought home. But Jesus says \"they will welcome you into eternal dwellings.\" (Luke 16:9) The wealth is gone, like the cookie off my counter. The dwellings are eternal."]},{"t":"story","text":"Jesus goes on, \"Whoever is {{faithful|faithful}} with very little will also be faithful with much,\" and then He flips it around. Friend, I got my stamp right one crooked cookie at a time.","ref":"Luke 16:10"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says someone who is dishonest with very little can still be honest with much.","ref":"Luke 16:10","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["That's the flip Jesus does, and it goes the other way. He says \"whoever is dishonest with very little will also be dishonest with much.\" (Luke 16:10) Dishonest with a little stays dishonest when the stakes get bigger. I wish crooked got straight just by making a bigger batch. Trust me, I tried. MORE crooked cookies.","You got it! Jesus says, \"Whoever is faithful with very little will also be faithful with much, and whoever is dishonest with very little will also be dishonest with much.\" (Luke 16:10) How you handle the little things shows up in the big things. Same way I got my stamp right, one crooked cookie at a time."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus asks a question about {{worldly wealth|worldly wealth}}. The kind of question that follows you home and sits down on your couch.","ref":"Luke 16:11"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus asks who will trust you with something greater if you haven't been {{faithful|faithful}} with {{worldly wealth|worldly wealth}}. What is that greater thing?","ref":"Luke 16:11","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["True riches","A kingdom of your own","Bigger storehouses"],"correct":0,"expl":["True riches! Jesus asks, \"So if you have not been faithful with worldly wealth, who will entrust you with true riches?\" (Luke 16:11) He sets worldly wealth right next to true riches and lets you sit with it. Told you that question follows you home and takes the good spot on the couch.","Not a kingdom, baker. Jesus asks, \"who will entrust you with true riches?\" (Luke 16:11) No crown in this verse. He puts worldly wealth next to true riches, asks His question, and lets it land.","Bigger storehouses would just be more worldly wealth with a bigger roof on it! Jesus asks \"who will entrust you with true riches?\" (Luke 16:11) Not more of the same stuff. A bigger cookie box is still just a cookie box, friend. Jesus is talking about something worth a whole lot more."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus takes it one step further with another question, and this one is about belongings.","ref":"Luke 16:12"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus asks: if you have not been {{faithful|faithful}} with the belongings of ___, who will give you belongings of your ___?","ref":"Luke 16:12","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["another","own","strangers","rulers","family"],"blanks":2,"correctSet":[0,1],"correct":0,"expl":["Another fills the first blank. Jesus asks, \"And if you have not been faithful with the belongings of another, who will give you belongings of your own?\" (Luke 16:12) Another means anybody besides you. It's the borrowed stuff, the thing you're only holding for a while, and Jesus asks about being faithful with that before He gets to anything of your own.","Own nails the second blank: \"who will give you belongings of your own?\" (Luke 16:12) Somebody else's things first, then your own. It's like me holding your cookie box, friend. Hand it back full of crumbs, and see if anybody trusts me with a box of my own.","Strangers would narrow it down to people you don't know, and Jesus never narrows it. He asks about \"the belongings of another\" (Luke 16:12). A stranger, a neighbor, the guy at the next desk, the verse doesn't pick one. If it's not yours, it counts.","Rulers would turn this into a palace story, and Jesus keeps it everyday. His word is another: \"the belongings of another\" (Luke 16:12). No throne room needed, baker. I put on my fanciest apron for a king, and it turns out the verse is about anybody's stuff at all.","Family makes it feel close to home, and I love that, but the word is another. Jesus asks about \"the belongings of another\" (Luke 16:12), and He never says whether that's a cousin, a coworker or the boss. He leaves the owner wide open."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Jesus lands it with a line about servants: \"No servant can serve two masters.\" Friend, nobody shows up to the big game wearing BOTH teams' jerseys.","ref":"Luke 16:13"},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says no servant can serve two masters. What does He say happens to a servant who tries?","ref":"Luke 16:13","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["He'll hate one and love the other","He'll run out of time for both","He'll get paid by neither one"],"correct":0,"expl":["That's it! \"Either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise the other.\" (Luke 16:13) No splitting it down the middle. One of those jerseys is coming off, friend.","Time isn't what Jesus talks about. He says, \"Either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise the other.\" (Luke 16:13) Love and hate, devotion and despising. That's not a crowded calendar, baker. That's picking a side.","Jesus never brings up the paycheck. He says the servant \"will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise the other.\" (Luke 16:13) It's loyalty, not payroll. You can't wear both jerseys to the game, no matter who's signing the checks."]},{"t":"choice","q":"Jesus says a person can serve both God and money, as long as they keep the two in balance.","ref":"Luke 16:13","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["No balancing act here, friend. Jesus says it plain: \"You cannot serve both God and money.\" (Luke 16:13) Not \"cannot unless you're careful.\" CANNOT. I tried carrying two cookie trays in two directions once, and I'm still finding crumbs.","Exactly. Jesus says, \"You cannot serve both God and money.\" (Luke 16:13) Not with a schedule, not with a budget, not with a perfect balance. Two masters, and only one gets served."]}]},{"id":"b","kind":"burnt","title":"Burnt Cookies","intro":"One last retelling, friend. Two of these cookies stayed in my oven too long, so each one has a detail that's wrong. Tap the 2 burnt ones.","lines":[{"text":"Jesus tells His disciples about a rich man whose manager is accused of wasting his possessions. The boss calls him in: turn in an account of your management, because you can't be manager any longer. Friend, that is the meeting NOBODY wants on their calendar.","ref":"Luke 16:1–2","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"The manager talks it over with himself. Too weak to dig, too ashamed to beg. Then it clicks, and he lands on a plan so that after he's removed, people will welcome him into their homes.","ref":"Luke 16:3–4","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"He calls in his master's debtors one by one. The first owes a hundred measures of wine, and the manager says sit down QUICKLY and write fifty. The next owes a hundred measures of wheat, and now it's eighty. Baker, that's the fastest markdown I've ever seen.","ref":"Luke 16:5–7","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"a hundred measures of wine","true":"a hundred measures of olive oil","ref":"Luke 16:6"}]},{"text":"The master commends the dishonest manager because he acted shrewdly. Then Jesus says to use worldly wealth to make friends, so when it's gone, they'll welcome you into eternal dwellings.","ref":"Luke 16:8–9","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Faithful with very little means faithful with much. If you're not faithful with worldly wealth, who'll trust you with ten cities? No servant can serve two masters, and Jesus lands it: \"You cannot serve both God and money.\"","ref":"Luke 16:10–13","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"ten cities","true":"true riches","ref":"Luke 16:11"}]}]},{"id":"e","kind":"ending","title":"The Big Idea","lesson":"","bigIdea":["Here's the big idea, friend. Jesus tells a story about a manager the text flat-out calls dishonest (Luke 16:8), and He uses it to teach His disciples about money. Money runs out, so use it with the end in view, and don't ever let it be your master (Luke 16:1-13).","What gets praised is NOT his honesty. The master commends him because he had acted shrewdly (Luke 16:8). He saw the end coming and got ready for it (Luke 16:4). Then the line right after says, \"the sons of this age are more shrewd in dealing with their own kind than are the sons of light.\" (Luke 16:8) Baker, that's your coworker with a five-year plan, while I'm over here still looking for my other oven mitt.","So Jesus says to use worldly wealth to make friends for yourselves, so that \"when it is gone,\" they will welcome you into eternal dwellings (Luke 16:9). WHEN it is gone. Not if. Money runs out. So spend the stuff that runs out on the stuff that lasts forever.","And no, Jesus is not handing out gold stars for creative math. He says whoever is dishonest with very little will also be dishonest with much (Luke 16:10). Then He asks it straight: if you haven't been faithful with worldly wealth, who will entrust you with true riches (Luke 16:11)?","Friend, how you handle the little stuff, even stuff that belongs to somebody else, shows what you'd do with the big stuff (Luke 16:10-12). Think about it. If I sneak cookies out of a box I'm holding for you, you are NOT handing me the keys to your bakery.","Then Jesus lands it: \"You cannot serve both God and money.\" (Luke 16:13) Money can be a tool in your hand. It cannot be the boss of your heart. One master. This is NOT a job share. And Jesus isn't done talking about money yet."]}],"words":{"disciples":{"word":"disciples","meaning":"People who followed Jesus so they could learn from Him and live the way He taught.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"accused":{"word":"accused","meaning":"Blamed by someone for doing something wrong.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"possessions":{"word":"possessions","meaning":"The things a person owns, like money, land, and goods.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"account":{"word":"account","meaning":"A written report showing how someone handled money or property that was put in their care.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"position":{"word":"position","meaning":"A person's job or role, the place they hold in someone's work.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"debtors":{"word":"debtors","meaning":"People who owe something, such as money or goods, to someone else.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"measures":{"word":"measures","meaning":"Set amounts used to count goods. Here the oil was counted in a unit of about 8 or 9 gallons, so a hundred of them was a huge amount. The wheat was counted in a much bigger unit, so a hundred of those was about 30 tons.","sources":["BSB footnote"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"barley":{"word":"barley","meaning":"A grain a lot like wheat, grown for food and often made into bread.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"bill":{"word":"bill","meaning":"A paper that writes down how much a person owes.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"debts":{"word":"debts","meaning":"Things a person owes and has to pay back.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"commend":{"word":"commend","meaning":"To praise someone or speak well of what they did.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"shrewd":{"word":"shrewd","meaning":"Quick and clever at seeing what to do to come out ahead.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"sons of this age":{"word":"sons of this age","meaning":"People who live only for this world and go by its ways.","sources":["Barnes' Notes on the Bible"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"sons of light":{"word":"sons of light","meaning":"People who belong to God and have been shown His truth.","sources":["Barnes' Notes on the Bible"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"worldly wealth":{"word":"worldly wealth","meaning":"Money and riches of this world, the kind people often chase and that does not last.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"eternal dwellings":{"word":"eternal dwellings","meaning":"Homes that last forever, meaning a place with God in heaven.","sources":["Barnes' Notes on the Bible"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"courts":{"word":"courts","meaning":"The palace of a king along with the officials and helpers who serve there.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"ivory":{"word":"ivory","meaning":"The hard, creamy white material from elephant tusks. It was costly and used to decorate rich people's houses.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"faithful":{"word":"faithful","meaning":"Able to be trusted to do what you are supposed to do, again and again.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"true riches":{"word":"true riches","meaning":"Not more money, but the real treasure that comes from God, like His grace and life with Him forever. It is worth far more than any wealth in this world.","sources":["Barnes' Notes on the Bible"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"storehouses":{"word":"storehouses","meaning":"Buildings where grain and other goods are kept and saved up.","sources":["Webster's Dictionary (1828)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"}}},{"id":"r5-acts13","type":"Narrative","title":"Paul Preaches in Pisidian Antioch","ref":"Acts 13:13–52","round":5,"parts":[{"id":"s1","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 1","range":"Acts 13:13–15","verses":[{"ref":"Acts 13:13","n":13,"text":"After setting sail from Paphos, Paul and his companions came to Perga in Pamphylia, where John left them to return to Jerusalem."},{"ref":"Acts 13:14","n":14,"text":"And from Perga, they traveled inland to Pisidian Antioch, where they entered the synagogue on the Sabbath and sat down."},{"ref":"Acts 13:15","n":15,"text":"After the reading from the Law and the Prophets, the synagogue leaders sent word to them: “Brothers, if you have a word of encouragement for the people, please speak.”"}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Friend, we're on the road with Paul and his companions. They set sail from Paphos and land at Perga in Pamphylia. And right there, their helper John leaves them.","ref":"Acts 13:13"},{"t":"choice","q":"John leaves Paul and his companions at Perga. Where does he go?","ref":"Acts 13:13","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Back to Jerusalem","Home to Tarsus","Down to Joppa"],"correct":0,"expl":["Jerusalem it is. At Perga, John left them “to return to Jerusalem” (Acts 13:13). Why? The verse doesn't say, and I'm not about to make something up. So the crew is one man short, and everybody else keeps moving, friend.","Tarsus doesn't show up on this trip, friend. The verse says John left them at Perga “to return to Jerusalem” (Acts 13:13). So he heads back to Jerusalem while Paul and the others keep going. Don't worry about the map. I once got lost walking from my oven to my cookie box, and that's FOUR steps.","Joppa's not where this road goes, friend. John heads back to Jerusalem from Perga, and the others keep traveling without him (Acts 13:13). Hold on to that name John, because Paul is going to mention a different John later, and I don't want you mixing them up like I mix up my sprinkle jars."]},{"t":"story","text":"The rest of them travel inland from Perga to Pisidian Antioch, and I get winded walking from my oven to the cookie box. Then the {{Sabbath|Sabbath}} comes around.","ref":"Acts 13:14"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: On the {{Sabbath|Sabbath}} in Pisidian Antioch, Paul and his companions go into the {{synagogue|synagogue}} and sit down.","ref":"Acts 13:14","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["That's right! In Pisidian Antioch, they “entered the synagogue on the Sabbath and sat down” (Acts 13:14). After that long trip inland from Perga, I'd sit down too. Shoot, I'd sit down and ask for a pillow. But they're not just resting, friend. Sitting in that synagogue puts them right where the next thing happens.","It's true, friend. After the trip inland from Perga, they “entered the synagogue on the Sabbath and sat down” (Acts 13:14). Think about it. You walk ALL that way, you find the synagogue, and you take a seat. That's exactly what they do, and that seat is about to turn into a pulpit."]},{"t":"story","text":"So there they sit. First comes the reading from {{the Law and the Prophets|the Law and the Prophets}}, and then the {{synagogue|synagogue}} leaders send word over to them.","ref":"Acts 13:15"},{"t":"choice","q":"The {{synagogue|synagogue}} leaders tell Paul and his companions, “Brothers, if you have a word of ___ for the people, please speak.”","ref":"Acts 13:15","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["encouragement","warning","prophecy"],"correct":0,"expl":["Encouragement, friend! The synagogue leaders send word, “Brothers, if you have a word of encouragement for the people, please speak” (Acts 13:15). They read from the Law and the Prophets, then they open the floor to the visitors. That's an open mic, and Paul is about to take it.","Nope, no warning. The leaders ask for a word of encouragement (Acts 13:15). Friend, nobody walks up to a guest speaker and says, “Hey, could you scare us a little?” Not if they ever want a guest speaker again.","I get it. The Prophets were JUST read out loud, so that word was still hanging in the air (Acts 13:15). But what the leaders ask the visitors for is a word of encouragement, something for the people. And friend, Paul does NOT need to be asked twice. He's on his feet in the very next verse (Acts 13:16)."]}]},{"id":"s2","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 2","range":"Acts 13:16–41","verses":[{"ref":"Acts 13:16","n":16,"text":"Paul stood up, motioned with his hand, and began to speak: “Men of Israel and you Gentiles who fear God, listen to me!"},{"ref":"Acts 13:17","n":17,"text":"The God of the people of Israel chose our fathers. He made them into a great people during their stay in Egypt, and with an uplifted arm He led them out of that land."},{"ref":"Acts 13:18","n":18,"text":"He endured their conduct for about forty years in the wilderness."},{"ref":"Acts 13:19","n":19,"text":"And having vanquished seven nations in Canaan, He gave their land to His people as an inheritance."},{"ref":"Acts 13:20","n":20,"text":"All this took about 450 years. After this, God gave them judges until the time of Samuel the prophet."},{"ref":"Acts 13:21","n":21,"text":"Then the people asked for a king, and God gave them Saul son of Kish, from the tribe of Benjamin, who ruled forty years."},{"ref":"Acts 13:22","n":22,"text":"After removing Saul, He raised up David as their king and testified about him: ‘I have found David son of Jesse a man after My own heart; he will carry out My will in its entirety.’"},{"ref":"Acts 13:23","n":23,"text":"From the descendants of this man, God has brought to Israel the Savior Jesus, as He promised."},{"ref":"Acts 13:24","n":24,"text":"Before the arrival of Jesus, John preached a baptism of repentance to all the people of Israel."},{"ref":"Acts 13:25","n":25,"text":"As John was completing his course, he said, ‘Who do you suppose I am? I am not that One. But there is One coming after me whose sandals I am not worthy to untie.’"},{"ref":"Acts 13:26","n":26,"text":"Brothers, children of Abraham, and you Gentiles who fear God, it is to us that this message of salvation has been sent."},{"ref":"Acts 13:27","n":27,"text":"The people of Jerusalem and their rulers did not recognize Jesus, yet in condemning Him they fulfilled the words of the prophets that are read every Sabbath."},{"ref":"Acts 13:28","n":28,"text":"And though they found no ground for a death sentence, they asked Pilate to have Him executed."},{"ref":"Acts 13:29","n":29,"text":"When they had carried out all that was written about Him, they took Him down from the tree and laid Him in a tomb."},{"ref":"Acts 13:30","n":30,"text":"But God raised Him from the dead,"},{"ref":"Acts 13:31","n":31,"text":"and for many days He was seen by those who had accompanied Him from Galilee to Jerusalem. They are now His witnesses to our people."},{"ref":"Acts 13:32","n":32,"text":"And now we proclaim to you the good news: What God promised our fathers"},{"ref":"Acts 13:33","n":33,"text":"He has fulfilled for us, their children, by raising up Jesus. As it is written in the second Psalm: ‘You are My Son; today I have become Your Father.’"},{"ref":"Acts 13:34","n":34,"text":"In fact, God raised Him from the dead, never to see decay. As He has said: ‘I will give you the holy and sure blessings promised to David.’"},{"ref":"Acts 13:35","n":35,"text":"So also, He says in another Psalm: ‘You will not let Your Holy One see decay.’"},{"ref":"Acts 13:36","n":36,"text":"For when David had served God’s purpose in his own generation, he fell asleep. His body was buried with his fathers and saw decay."},{"ref":"Acts 13:37","n":37,"text":"But the One whom God raised from the dead did not see decay."},{"ref":"Acts 13:38","n":38,"text":"Therefore let it be known to you, brothers, that through Jesus the forgiveness of sins is proclaimed to you."},{"ref":"Acts 13:39","n":39,"text":"Through Him everyone who believes is justified from everything from which you could not be justified by the law of Moses."},{"ref":"Acts 13:40","n":40,"text":"Watch out, then, that what was spoken by the prophets does not happen to you:"},{"ref":"Acts 13:41","n":41,"text":"‘Look, you scoffers, wonder and perish! For I am doing a work in your days that you would never believe, even if someone told you.’”"}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Paul stands up, motions with his hand and begins: “Men of Israel and you {{Gentiles|Gentiles}} who {{fear God|fear God}}, listen to me!” No opening joke like I'd tell, friend: he goes WAY back to how the God of Israel chose their fathers, made them a great people in Egypt and led them out with an {{uplifted arm|uplifted arm}}.","ref":"Acts 13:16–17"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: In Paul's history of Israel, God endures the people's conduct in the {{wilderness|wilderness}} for about forty years.","ref":"Acts 13:18","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":0,"expl":["True! Paul says of God, “He endured their conduct for about forty years in the wilderness” (Acts 13:18). Forty years, friend. I lose patience waiting forty SECONDS for my oven to preheat. God's patience with His people is a whole different level.","Oh, it's true. Forty YEARS of it out in the wilderness (Acts 13:18). And after all that, God still gives His people the land as an inheritance (Acts 13:19). Friend, I can't put up with my own humming for forty minutes."]},{"t":"story","text":"God {{vanquishes|vanquishes}} seven nations in {{Canaan|Canaan}} and gives their land to His people as an {{inheritance|inheritance}}. All that takes about 450 years, and then God gives them {{judges|judges}} until the time of Samuel the prophet.","ref":"Acts 13:19–20"},{"t":"choice","q":"After the time of the {{judges|judges}}, the people of Israel ask for a ___.","ref":"Acts 13:21","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["king","temple","prophet"],"correct":0,"expl":["A king, friend. Paul says, “Then the people asked for a king,” and God gave them Saul son of Kish (Acts 13:21). They've had judges, they've had Samuel the prophet, and now they want a crown on somebody. And God answers that request.","No construction project here. “Then the people asked for a king” (Acts 13:21), and God gave them Saul son of Kish, from the tribe of Benjamin. Paul's history goes from judges to crowns, not bricks. Which is a shame, friend, because I'd have volunteered to stamp every single brick.","Good guess, since Paul just name-dropped Samuel the prophet. But Samuel is where the judges end (Acts 13:20). What comes next is “Then the people asked for a king” (Acts 13:21). Judges, prophet, king. Friend, Paul is flying through Israel's history so fast I'd need a cookie timer just to keep up."]},{"t":"story","text":"God gives them Saul son of Kish, from the {{tribe|tribe}} of Benjamin, and Saul rules forty years. Then God removes Saul, raises up a new king and {{testifies|testifies}} about him: “{{idiom:921|a man after My own heart}}; he will carry out My will in its entirety.”","ref":"Acts 13:21–22"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says God removed Saul. Who does God raise up as Israel's king after him?","ref":"Acts 13:22","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["David son of Jesse","Saul's son Jonathan","Samuel the prophet"],"correct":0,"expl":["David, friend! After removing Saul, God “raised up David as their king” and called him “a man after My own heart” (Acts 13:22). And keep your eye on David, because Paul isn't done with him. He's about to connect David to the Savior.","Jonathan doesn't even get a mention in Paul's sermon. Paul goes straight from removing Saul to “He raised up David as their king” (Acts 13:22). No stops in between, friend. That's me going from the oven to the cookie box when the batch smells THAT good.","Samuel's the prophet, and Paul puts him at the end of the time of the judges (Acts 13:20). The crown goes to David (Acts 13:22). Samuel as king would be like me trying to be the cookie box, friend. Great at my job. Not THAT job."]},{"t":"story","text":"From David's descendants, God has brought Israel the Savior Jesus, as He promised. Before Jesus arrived, John preached a {{baptism of repentance|baptism of repentance}} to all the people of Israel, and no, not the John who left them at Perga.","ref":"Acts 13:23–24"},{"t":"choice","q":"As John is completing his {{course|course}}, he says One is coming after him whose ___ he is not worthy to untie.","ref":"Acts 13:25","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["sandals","belt","robe"],"correct":0,"expl":["Sandals! John says, “But there is One coming after me whose sandals I am not worthy to untie” (Acts 13:25). Not worthy to untie them. That's humility, friend. I get a little proud when my sprinkles land straight. And the One John is pointing to is Jesus.","Lower, friend. All the way down to the feet. John says he's not worthy to untie the sandals of the One coming after him, and that One is Jesus (Acts 13:25). Belts, I can't help you with anyway. I don't wear one. My apron strings do all the work, and they are working HARD.","Look down, way down. It's the sandals John says he's not worthy to untie (Acts 13:25). He's pointing past himself to the One coming after him, and that's Jesus. Me, I point with my mustache, friend, and half the time I knock over a sprinkle jar doing it."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Paul tells his listeners that it is to us this message of {{salvation|salvation}} has been sent. The people of Jerusalem and their rulers didn't recognize Jesus, yet in {{condemning|condemning}} Him they {{fulfilled|fulfilled}} the words of the prophets read every {{Sabbath|Sabbath}}.","ref":"Acts 13:26–27"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: Paul says the people of Jerusalem and their rulers found solid ground for a death sentence against Jesus.","ref":"Acts 13:28","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["They came up empty, friend. No ground for a death sentence. Not a crumb of a reason. And they still went to Pilate, the governor of Judea (Luke 3:1), and asked him to have Jesus executed (Acts 13:28).","False is right. Paul says they “found no ground for a death sentence” (Acts 13:28). Let that sink in, friend. They had NOTHING on Him, and they still asked Pilate, the governor of Judea (Luke 3:1), to have Him executed (Acts 13:28)."]},{"t":"story","text":"No ground at all, and they still asked {{Pilate|Pilate}} to have Him executed. Once they had carried out all that was written about Him, they took Him down from the {{tree|tree}} and laid Him in a tomb.","ref":"Acts 13:28–29"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: Paul tells the {{synagogue|synagogue}} that Jesus stayed in the tomb where they laid Him after taking Him down from the {{tree|tree}}.","ref":"Acts 13:30","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["That tomb was not His forever address, friend. Paul says it plain: “But God raised Him from the dead” (Acts 13:30). They laid Him in a tomb (Acts 13:29), and God raised Him. That's the turning point of Paul's whole sermon.","False! After they laid Him in a tomb (Acts 13:29), Paul says, “But God raised Him from the dead” (Acts 13:30). That “But God” is my favorite little phrase, friend. Everything was going one way, and then BUT GOD."]},{"t":"story","text":"That tomb didn't keep Him, friend. God raised Him from the dead, and for many days He was seen.","ref":"Acts 13:30–31"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says that after God raised Jesus from the dead, He was seen for many days. Who saw Him?","ref":"Acts 13:31","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Those who came with Him from Galilee","The rulers who condemned Him in Jerusalem","The synagogue leaders of Pisidian Antioch"],"correct":0,"expl":["Yes, friend! Paul says He was seen “by those who had accompanied Him from Galilee to Jerusalem” (Acts 13:31). And those people are “now His witnesses to our people” (Acts 13:31). They walked with Him, they saw Him alive again, and now they tell it.","Not the rulers, friend. Paul says those rulers didn't even recognize Jesus (Acts 13:27). After God raised Him, He was seen “by those who had accompanied Him from Galilee to Jerusalem” (Acts 13:31). The ones who walked with Him are the ones who saw Him, and now they're His witnesses.","These folks are hearing about it from Paul, friend, not seeing it themselves. Paul says Jesus was seen “by those who had accompanied Him from Galilee to Jerusalem” (Acts 13:31). The leaders in Antioch are the ones who asked for a word of encouragement (Acts 13:15). And boy, are they getting one."]},{"t":"story","text":"That's the good news, Paul says: what God promised their fathers, He has {{fulfilled|fulfilled}} for their children by raising up Jesus. Then Paul pulls from the {{Psalms|Psalms}}, including this one: “You will not let Your {{Holy One|Holy One}} see {{decay|decay}}.”","ref":"Acts 13:32–35"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says David served God's purpose in his generation, {{fell asleep|fell asleep}}, was buried and saw {{decay|decay}}. So who does Paul say did not {{idiom:472|see decay}}?","ref":"Acts 13:36–37","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Jesus the Savior of Israel","Samuel the prophet of Israel","Saul son of Kish from Benjamin"],"correct":0,"expl":["Jesus, friend! Paul says, “But the One whom God raised from the dead did not see decay” (Acts 13:37). David served, died and saw decay (Acts 13:36), so that Psalm about the Holy One can't be about David. It's about Jesus, the Savior God brought to Israel (Acts 13:23). That's Paul connecting the dots like a pro.","Paul's only got two names on the board here, and Samuel isn't one of them. David saw decay (Acts 13:36), but the One God raised from the dead did not, and that's Jesus (Acts 13:37). Picking Samuel here is like me grabbing the sprinkle jar when the recipe says chocolate chips, friend. Good jar. Wrong cookie.","Saul left this sermon back when God removed him (Acts 13:22), and Paul isn't bringing him back for an encore. The One who did not see decay is the One God raised from the dead (Acts 13:37), and God raised Jesus (Acts 13:30). Friend, I know a thing or two about getting slid to the back of the drawer and staying there."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Paul brings it home: through Jesus, the forgiveness of sins is {{proclaimed|proclaimed}} to them. When my stamps came out crooked, I slid behind the takeout menus, friend, and nobody PROCLAIMED a thing.","ref":"Acts 13:38"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says that through Jesus, everyone who believes is {{justified|justified}} from everything they could not be justified from by the law of ___.","ref":"Acts 13:39","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["Moses","Abraham","David"],"correct":0,"expl":["Moses, friend! Paul says that through Jesus “everyone who believes is justified from everything from which you could not be justified by the law of Moses” (Acts 13:39). Everything, friend. EVERYTHING. What the law couldn't do for them, Jesus does for everyone who believes.","Abraham's in the sermon, but not in this line, friend. Paul calls them “children of Abraham” (Acts 13:26), but the law he names is the law of Moses (Acts 13:39). Through Jesus, everyone who believes is justified from everything the law of Moses couldn't justify them from. That's the good news Paul is preaching.","David gets a LOT of airtime in this sermon, so I see why you went there, friend. But the law Paul names is “the law of Moses” (Acts 13:39). Through Jesus, everyone who believes is justified from everything that law couldn't justify them from. David's the king, Moses is the law."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Paul ends with a warning: watch out that what the prophets spoke doesn't happen to you. It speaks of a work done in their days that they would never believe, even if someone told them.","ref":"Acts 13:40–41"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul's warning from the prophets tells some people to look, wonder and {{perish|perish}}. Who does it call out?","ref":"Acts 13:40–41","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Scoffers","Idol makers","False shepherds"],"correct":0,"expl":["Scoffers it is. Paul quotes the prophets: “Look, you scoffers, wonder and perish!” (Acts 13:41). Then the kicker: a work done in their days that they would never believe, even if someone told them (Acts 13:41). And Paul's last word to his listeners? Watch out that this doesn't happen to you (Acts 13:40). Friend, that's how you end a sermon.","No idols in this warning. The prophets' words Paul quotes call out scoffers (Acts 13:41), people who'd hear about a work done in their own days and still refuse to believe it. Friend, I'd make a terrible scoffer anyway. Tell me ANYTHING about cookies and I believe it on the spot.","It's the scoffers, friend. Paul quotes, “Look, you scoffers, wonder and perish!” (Acts 13:41). These are folks who wouldn't believe a work done in their own days, even if someone told them (Acts 13:41). That's me with a new cookie recipe. “That'll never work.” Then it works, and I look silly."]}]},{"id":"s3","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 3","range":"Acts 13:42–52","verses":[{"ref":"Acts 13:42","n":42,"text":"As Paul and Barnabas were leaving the synagogue, the people urged them to continue this message on the next Sabbath."},{"ref":"Acts 13:43","n":43,"text":"After the synagogue was dismissed, many of the Jews and devout converts to Judaism followed Paul and Barnabas, who spoke to them and urged them to continue in the grace of God."},{"ref":"Acts 13:44","n":44,"text":"On the following Sabbath, nearly the whole city gathered to hear the word of the Lord."},{"ref":"Acts 13:45","n":45,"text":"But when the Jews saw the crowds, they were filled with jealousy, and they blasphemously contradicted what Paul was saying."},{"ref":"Acts 13:46","n":46,"text":"Then Paul and Barnabas answered them boldly: “It was necessary to speak the word of God to you first. But since you reject it and do not consider yourselves worthy of eternal life, we now turn to the Gentiles."},{"ref":"Acts 13:47","n":47,"text":"For this is what the Lord has commanded us: ‘I have made you a light for the Gentiles, to bring salvation to the ends of the earth.’”"},{"ref":"Acts 13:48","n":48,"text":"When the Gentiles heard this, they rejoiced and glorified the word of the Lord, and all who were appointed for eternal life believed."},{"ref":"Acts 13:49","n":49,"text":"And the word of the Lord spread throughout that region."},{"ref":"Acts 13:50","n":50,"text":"The Jews, however, incited the religious women of prominence and the leading men of the city. They stirred up persecution against Paul and Barnabas and drove them out of their district."},{"ref":"Acts 13:51","n":51,"text":"So they shook the dust off their feet in protest against them and went to Iconium."},{"ref":"Acts 13:52","n":52,"text":"And the disciples were filled with joy and with the Holy Spirit."}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Sermon's over. As Paul and his partner Barnabas are leaving the {{synagogue|synagogue}}, the people stop them.","ref":"Acts 13:42"},{"t":"choice","q":"What do the people urge Paul and Barnabas to do as they leave the {{synagogue|synagogue}}?","ref":"Acts 13:42","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Continue this message on the next Sabbath","Leave the city before the next Sabbath","Write this message down for the leaders"],"correct":0,"expl":["You got it, friend. The people urged them “to continue this message on the next Sabbath” (Acts 13:42). They want MORE. Paul just preached a whole sermon, and they're already asking for another one next week. That's the best review a preacher can get.","The opposite, friend! The people urged them “to continue this message on the next Sabbath” (Acts 13:42). They aren't showing Paul and Barnabas the door. They're asking for a part two.","They want to HEAR it again, friend. The people urged them “to continue this message on the next Sabbath” (Acts 13:42). Nobody asks for a written copy. They want Paul back in person next week, and that's a sign the message landed."]},{"t":"story","text":"After the {{synagogue|synagogue}} is dismissed, many of the Jews and {{devout|devout}} {{converts|converts}} to Judaism follow Paul and Barnabas. The two of them talk with these followers.","ref":"Acts 13:43"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul and Barnabas urge the Jews and {{converts|converts}} who followed them to continue in the ___ of God.","ref":"Acts 13:43","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["grace","law","house"],"correct":0,"expl":["Grace, friend! After the synagogue let out, many of the Jews and devout converts followed Paul and Barnabas, and the word they got was to “continue in the grace of God” (Acts 13:43). Keep going in it. Stay right there. That's me next to a fresh tray: as close as possible, for as long as possible.","Smart guess, since the law of Moses came up just a few verses back (Acts 13:39). But the send-off word is grace. Paul and Barnabas urge their followers to keep on in God's grace (Acts 13:43). Paul packs so much into one sermon, friend, I'd need to take notes. On a cookie. In chocolate chips.","No house keys being handed out here, friend. Paul and Barnabas urge the people following them “to continue in the grace of God” (Acts 13:43). Grace isn't an address. If it were, I'd have moved in already and parked my oven by the front door."]},{"t":"story","text":"The following {{Sabbath|Sabbath}}, NEARLY THE WHOLE CITY gathers to hear the word of the Lord. Friend, I can't get three bakers to show up for a taste test.","ref":"Acts 13:44"},{"t":"choice","q":"When the Jews see the crowds gathered to hear Paul, they are filled with ___.","ref":"Acts 13:45","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["jealousy","wonder","shame"],"correct":0,"expl":["Jealousy, friend. When the Jews saw the crowds, “they were filled with jealousy” (Acts 13:45). Nearly the whole city shows up, and instead of joy, it's jealousy. Remember, I can't get three bakers to a taste test. If nearly the whole city lined up for somebody else's cookies, I'd smile real big. Through clenched teeth. Okay, I might need a minute.","Funny, “wonder” was in Paul's warning about scoffers (Acts 13:41). But here, when the Jews saw the crowds, “they were filled with jealousy” (Acts 13:45). And it doesn't stay quiet, friend. They start contradicting what Paul is saying.","It's jealousy, friend. When the Jews saw the crowds, “they were filled with jealousy” (Acts 13:45). Nearly the whole city gathered to hear the word of the Lord (Acts 13:44). Not everyone is happy about that, and it shows."]},{"t":"story","text":"They {{blasphemously|blasphemously}} contradict what Paul is saying. But Paul and Barnabas answer them boldly: it was necessary to speak the word of God to them first.","ref":"Acts 13:45–46"},{"t":"choice","q":"Since those Jews reject the word and don't consider themselves worthy of {{eternal life|eternal life}}, who do Paul and Barnabas say they will turn to now?","ref":"Acts 13:46","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["The Gentiles","The rulers in Jerusalem","The people of Tarsus"],"correct":0,"expl":["The Gentiles! Paul and Barnabas say, “we now turn to the Gentiles” (Acts 13:46). They spoke the word to the Jews first, like they said they had to. Now the door swings wide open, friend. WIDE open.","Jerusalem's not on the itinerary. Since those Jews in Antioch reject the word, Paul and Barnabas turn to the Gentiles (Acts 13:46). That's a turn, friend, not a U-turn. Me, I make about forty U-turns a day between the oven and the cookie box.","Paul and Barnabas aren't naming a town at all. They're naming a whole people: “we now turn to the Gentiles” (Acts 13:46). Think bigger, friend. MUCH bigger. I get excited when I fill ONE cookie box."]},{"t":"story","text":"And they back it up with Scripture, saying this is what the Lord has commanded them. It's the Lord speaking, “I have made you a light for the {{Gentiles|Gentiles}},” and He's not done.","ref":"Acts 13:47"},{"t":"choice","q":"The Lord's words that Paul and Barnabas quote go on: “to bring {{salvation|salvation}} to the ___ of the earth.”","ref":"Acts 13:47","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["ends","corners","nations"],"correct":0,"expl":["The ends of the earth! The Lord's words go, “I have made you a light for the Gentiles, to bring salvation to the ends of the earth” (Acts 13:47). Friend, I've got one little cookie planet, and I still lose things on it. The Lord's talking about the whole EARTH, all the way to the edges.","Close, friend, but the word is ends. The Lord says, “I have made you a light for the Gentiles, to bring salvation to the ends of the earth” (Acts 13:47). Same big idea: salvation going as far as the earth goes. Paul and Barnabas say this is what the Lord commanded them.","The word is ends, friend. The Lord says, “I have made you a light for the Gentiles, to bring salvation to the ends of the earth” (Acts 13:47). The Gentiles are already named in the first half. The second half sends salvation as far as the earth goes."]},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul and Barnabas say they are turning to the {{Gentiles|Gentiles}}, and they quote the line about a light for the Gentiles. How do the Gentiles in Pisidian Antioch react when they hear it?","ref":"Acts 13:48","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["They celebrate and praise the Lord's message","They hesitate and ask for a sign first","They go quiet and wait for their leaders"],"correct":0,"expl":["Party in Pisidian Antioch! When the Gentiles heard this, “they rejoiced and glorified the word of the Lord” (Acts 13:48). They just heard the door is open for them, friend. You'd better believe they're celebrating.","No hesitating here, friend. When the Gentiles heard this, “they rejoiced and glorified the word of the Lord” (Acts 13:48). They don't ask for a sign. They hear the message and they CELEBRATE.","Quiet? Not even close, friend. When the Gentiles heard this, “they rejoiced and glorified the word of the Lord” (Acts 13:48). Nobody's waiting around for permission. They're rejoicing right then and there."]},{"t":"story","text":"And all who were {{appointed|appointed}} for {{eternal life|eternal life}} believed. Whole new batch of believers, fresh out of the oven.","ref":"Acts 13:48"},{"t":"choice","q":"True or false: The word of the Lord stays inside the city of Pisidian Antioch.","ref":"Acts 13:49","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["It gets out, friend! The verse says, “And the word of the Lord spread throughout that region” (Acts 13:49). Not just the city. The whole region. Kind of like when I sneak one cookie and somehow the whole bakery knows before the next batch is out.","False! “And the word of the Lord spread throughout that region” (Acts 13:49). One synagogue sermon, then nearly the whole city, and now the whole region. Friend, that message is MOVING."]},{"t":"story","text":"But the Jews {{incite|incite}} the religious women of {{prominence|prominence}} and the leading men of the city. Uh oh.","ref":"Acts 13:50"},{"t":"choice","q":"{{persecution|Persecution}} is stirred up against Paul and Barnabas. What happens to the two of them?","ref":"Acts 13:50","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["They are driven out of the district","They are locked in the city jail","They are sent back to Jerusalem"],"correct":0,"expl":["Driven out, friend. They “stirred up persecution against Paul and Barnabas and drove them out of their district” (Acts 13:50). One Sabbath nearly the whole city shows up, and then they get pushed out of the district. But hold on, because they're not done.","No jail cell in this one. Nobody locks them in. They get pushed OUT of the district (Acts 13:50). Friend, I know a little about getting pushed aside. I got slid behind the takeout menus. At least Paul and Barnabas got a whole open road.","Nobody ships them back to Jerusalem. The persecution gets stirred up, and they're driven out of the district, period (Acts 13:50). No escort, no return ticket. Friend, if somebody pushed me that hard, I'd roll right off my little planet."]},{"t":"story","text":"So Paul and Barnabas {{shake the dust off their feet|shake the dust off their feet}} in protest against them. Then they hit the road again.","ref":"Acts 13:51"},{"t":"choice","q":"With the dust shaken off their feet, Paul and Barnabas go to ___.","ref":"Acts 13:51","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["Iconium","Ephesus","Corinth"],"correct":0,"expl":["Iconium! They “shook the dust off their feet in protest against them and went to Iconium” (Acts 13:51). Driven out of one district, and they just keep walking. Friend, if somebody chased me out of my bakery, I'd need a nap and a whole box of cookies. These two shake it off and head for the NEXT town.","Not Ephesus. They shake the dust off their feet and go to Iconium (Acts 13:51). Shake, stomp, walk. I tried shaking the dust off my feet once, friend, and I flung sprinkles across the entire bakery.","Corinth's not the stop. Iconium is (Acts 13:51). Look how they leave: they shake the dust off their feet in protest against them, and they go. When I get upset, friend, I don't shake anything off. I sulk. LOUDLY. Into a cookie box."]},{"t":"story","text":"And the {{disciples|disciples}} are filled with joy and with the {{Holy Spirit|Holy Spirit}}. Keep your apron on, friend, because this road trip is far from over.","ref":"Acts 13:52"}]},{"id":"b","kind":"burnt","title":"Burnt Cookies","intro":"One last retelling, friend. Two of these cookies stayed in my oven too long, so each one has a detail that's wrong. Tap the 2 burnt ones.","lines":[{"text":"John heads back to Jerusalem, but the rest reach Pisidian Antioch. On the Sabbath, the synagogue leaders ask: any word of encouragement?","ref":"Acts 13:13–15","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Paul stands up and gives them the WHOLE history: Egypt, forty years in the wilderness, the judges, then King Saul son of Kish from the tribe of Judah, then David son of Jesse. Then comes John, not worthy to untie the sandals of the One coming after him.","ref":"Acts 13:16–25","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"Saul is from the tribe of Judah","true":"Saul is from the tribe of Benjamin","ref":"Acts 13:21"}]},{"text":"Jerusalem's rulers find no ground for a death sentence, and they still ask Pilate to have Jesus executed. But God raises Him from the dead, and He does not see decay. Through Him, everyone who believes is justified from everything the law of Moses couldn't justify them from. Then a warning for the scoffers.","ref":"Acts 13:26–41","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Next Sabbath, nearly the whole city shows up. The Jews are filled with jealousy and contradict Paul. Then Paul and Silas answer them boldly: we now turn to the Gentiles. And the Gentiles? They REJOICE.","ref":"Acts 13:42–48","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"Paul's partner is Silas","true":"Paul's partner is Barnabas","ref":"Acts 13:46"}]},{"text":"The Jews stir up persecution, and Paul and Barnabas get driven out. They shake the dust off their feet and head to Iconium. And the disciples? Filled with joy and the Holy Spirit.","ref":"Acts 13:49–52","burnt":false,"wrong":[]}]},{"id":"e","kind":"ending","title":"The Big Idea","lesson":"","bigIdea":["Friend, Paul stands up in that synagogue and squeezes centuries of Israel's history into it: Egypt, the wilderness, the judges, the kings. And all of it leads to one name: Jesus, the Savior God promised from David's line (Acts 13:17-23).","Here's the difference Paul hangs everything on. David served God's purpose, fell asleep and saw decay. Jesus? God raised Him from the dead, and He did not see decay (Acts 13:36-37). God kept His promise to the fathers by raising Him up (Acts 13:32-33).","So what does that mean for you and me? Through Jesus, forgiveness of sins is proclaimed, and everyone who believes is justified from everything the law of Moses could never justify them from (Acts 13:38-39). Not \"everyone who stamps perfectly.\" EVERYONE WHO BELIEVES. As a stamper who spent years behind the takeout menus, friend, that's my favorite part of the whole sermon.","Then watch what happens. Same message, two reactions. Some people urge Paul and Barnabas to bring it back next Sabbath, but when some see the crowds, they are filled with jealousy and contradict what Paul is saying (Acts 13:42-45). Same batch of cookies: one person asks for seconds, the next one sends the plate back to the kitchen.","But rejection doesn't stop the word. It sends it OUTWARD, because this is what the Lord commanded them: “I have made you a light for the Gentiles, to bring salvation to the ends of the earth.” (Acts 13:46-47). The Gentiles rejoice, and the word of the Lord spreads throughout that region (Acts 13:48-49).","Paul and Barnabas face stirred-up persecution and get driven out of the district (Acts 13:50). And how does the chapter close? “And the disciples were filled with joy and with the Holy Spirit.” (Acts 13:52). Not a pouting ending, friend. JOY.","And Paul and Barnabas are already headed to Iconium (Acts 13:51). The road isn't done with them yet, friend."]}],"words":{"Sabbath":{"word":"Sabbath","meaning":"The seventh day of the Jewish week, set apart by God's law as a day of rest from work and a time for worship.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"synagogue":{"word":"synagogue","meaning":"A local Jewish meeting house where people gathered, especially on the Sabbath, to pray and to hear the Scriptures read and explained.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"the Law and the Prophets":{"word":"the Law and the Prophets","meaning":"A name for the Jewish Scriptures: the five books of Moses (the Law) together with the writings of the prophets. Passages from both were read aloud in the synagogue.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Gentiles":{"word":"Gentiles","meaning":"People who are not Jewish; the other nations of the world, as distinct from Israel.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"fear God":{"word":"fear God","meaning":"Here this means to honor and worship God with deep respect, not to be scared of Him. Gentiles who feared God worshiped Israel's God without fully becoming Jews.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"uplifted arm":{"word":"uplifted arm","meaning":"A picture of power in action. In the Bible the arm stands for strength, so God leading Israel out \"with an uplifted arm\" means He did it with open, mighty power.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"wilderness":{"word":"wilderness","meaning":"Wild, mostly empty land with few people and little farming. Here it is the desert where Israel wandered after leaving Egypt.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"vanquishes":{"word":"vanquishes","meaning":"Defeats completely; conquers.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Canaan":{"word":"Canaan","meaning":"The land west of the Jordan River that God promised to Abraham's descendants, later known as the land of Israel.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"inheritance":{"word":"inheritance","meaning":"Something passed down to someone to keep as their own. Here it is the land God handed over to Israel as their lasting possession.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"judges":{"word":"judges","meaning":"Leaders God raised up to rescue and govern Israel in the years after they settled in Canaan and before they had kings.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"tribe":{"word":"tribe","meaning":"One of the twelve family groups of Israel, each descended from one of Jacob's sons.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"testifies":{"word":"testifies","meaning":"Speaks up openly to say that something is true. Here God speaks out in favor of David and tells what kind of man he is.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"baptism of repentance":{"word":"baptism of repentance","meaning":"Washing with water that showed a person was turning away from their sins and back to God. Repentance means a real change of heart and life.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"course":{"word":"course","meaning":"The path or race of a person's life. John \"completing his course\" means he was nearing the end of his life's work.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"salvation":{"word":"salvation","meaning":"Being rescued by God, especially from sin and its punishment, and brought into life with Him.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"condemning":{"word":"condemning","meaning":"Declaring someone guilty and passing sentence on them.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"fulfilled":{"word":"fulfilled","meaning":"Made something come true or carried it all the way out. A prophecy or promise is fulfilled when what it said would happen really happens.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Pilate":{"word":"Pilate","meaning":"Pontius Pilate, the Roman governor of Judea who gave the order for Jesus to be crucified.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"tree":{"word":"tree","meaning":"Here it means the wooden cross Jesus was put to death on.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Psalms":{"word":"Psalms","meaning":"A book of the Old Testament made up of sacred songs and prayers, many of them written by King David.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Holy One":{"word":"Holy One","meaning":"A title for the one God has set apart as completely His own. In this Psalm it points ahead to the Messiah, God's chosen King, and Paul shows that it means Jesus.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"decay":{"word":"decay","meaning":"Rotting away. Here it means a dead body breaking down in the grave.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"fell asleep":{"word":"fell asleep","meaning":"A gentle way the Bible speaks of dying.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"proclaimed":{"word":"proclaimed","meaning":"Announced openly and publicly for everyone to hear.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"justified":{"word":"justified","meaning":"Declared right with God: cleared of guilt and accepted by Him as righteous.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"scoffers":{"word":"scoffers","meaning":"People who mock or sneer at something instead of taking it seriously.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"perish":{"word":"perish","meaning":"To die or be destroyed.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"devout":{"word":"devout","meaning":"Sincerely devoted to God and faithful in worship.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"converts":{"word":"converts","meaning":"People who have taken up a new religion. Here they are Gentiles who had become followers of the Jewish faith.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"grace":{"word":"grace","meaning":"God's kindness and favor given freely to people who have not earned it.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"blasphemously":{"word":"blasphemously","meaning":"In a way that insults or speaks evil against God and the things of God.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"eternal life":{"word":"eternal life","meaning":"Life that never ends; the lasting life with God that He gives to those who believe.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"appointed":{"word":"appointed","meaning":"Chosen or set apart ahead of time for something.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"incite":{"word":"incite","meaning":"To stir someone up and push them into action, often against someone else.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"prominence":{"word":"prominence","meaning":"High standing; being well known and important in the community.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"persecution":{"word":"persecution","meaning":"Harsh, ongoing mistreatment of people, especially because of what they believe.","sources":["Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary (1913)"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"shake the dust off their feet":{"word":"shake the dust off their feet","meaning":"A public sign of leaving a place and breaking ties with the people who refused the message, showing they were responsible for that choice.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"disciples":{"word":"disciples","meaning":"Followers who learn from a teacher. Here it means those who believed in and followed Jesus.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"Holy Spirit":{"word":"Holy Spirit","meaning":"The Spirit of God, who lives in believers and gives them strength, guidance and joy.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"idiom:472":{"word":"see decay","meaning":"To die and rot in the grave. To \"not see decay\" means to escape death and the grave.","sources":["Barnes","Jamieson-Fausset-Brown","Gill","Cambridge"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"},"idiom:916":{"word":"he fell asleep","meaning":"To have died. Sleep is the usual gentle picture for death, especially a believer's death.","sources":["Ellicott","Barnes","Jamieson-Fausset-Brown","Gill","Cambridge","Pulpit"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"},"idiom:921":{"word":"a man after My own heart","meaning":"A man God chose who fits what God wants, someone who would carry out God's will (David, in contrast to Saul).","sources":["Benson","Gill"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"},"idiom:922":{"word":"took Him down from the tree","meaning":"They took Jesus' body down from the cross (\"tree\" being a word for the cross) after His death.","sources":["Ellicott","Barnes","Gill"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"},"idiom:924":{"word":"saw decay","meaning":"To die and rot in the grave. To \"not see decay\" means to escape death and the grave.","sources":["Barnes","Jamieson-Fausset-Brown","Gill","Cambridge"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"},"idiom:925":{"word":"did not see decay","meaning":"To die and rot in the grave. To \"not see decay\" means to escape death and the grave.","sources":["Barnes","Jamieson-Fausset-Brown","Gill","Cambridge"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"},"idiom:926":{"word":"they shook the dust off their feet","meaning":"A public sign of breaking off from a place that rejected God's messengers; Jews shook off the dust of pagan lands as unclean, so doing this to a Jewish town treated it as no better than heathen, cut all ties, and left it responsible for its own guilt.","sources":["Ellicott","Benson","Barnes","Jamieson-Fausset-Brown","Gill","Cambridge","Pulpit"],"kindLabel":"Idiom on the path"}}},{"id":"r5-ephesians2","type":"Letters","title":"Alive with Christ by Grace","ref":"Ephesians 2:1–10","round":5,"parts":[{"id":"s1","kind":"walk","title":"Stop 1","range":"Ephesians 2:1–10","verses":[{"ref":"Ephesians 2:1","n":1,"text":"And you were dead in your trespasses and sins,"},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:2","n":2,"text":"in which you used to walk when you conformed to the ways of this world and of the ruler of the power of the air, the spirit who is now at work in the sons of disobedience."},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:3","n":3,"text":"All of us also lived among them at one time, fulfilling the cravings of our flesh and indulging its desires and thoughts. Like the rest, we were by nature children of wrath."},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:4","n":4,"text":"But because of His great love for us, God, who is rich in mercy,"},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:5","n":5,"text":"made us alive with Christ even when we were dead in our trespasses. It is by grace you have been saved!"},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:6","n":6,"text":"And God raised us up with Christ and seated us with Him in the heavenly realms in Christ Jesus,"},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:7","n":7,"text":"in order that in the coming ages He might display the surpassing riches of His grace, demonstrated by His kindness to us in Christ Jesus."},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:8","n":8,"text":"For it is by grace you have been saved through faith, and this not from yourselves; it is the gift of God,"},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:9","n":9,"text":"not by works, so that no one can boast."},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:10","n":10,"text":"For we are God’s workmanship, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance as our way of life."}],"cards":[{"t":"story","text":"Paul, an {{apostle|apostle}} of Christ Jesus, is writing to the believers in Ephesus, and here he reminds them where they started. Not sick, not sleepy, friend: dead in their {{trespasses|trespasses}} and sins. Back then they {{walked|walked}} in those sins.","ref":"Ephesians 2:1–2"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says that back when the believers in Ephesus {{walked|walked}} in their sins, they were just following their own way and nobody else's.","ref":"Ephesians 2:2","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["I get it, friend, walking in your sins sure sounds like a solo project. But Paul says they walked that way when they \"conformed to the ways of this world and of the ruler of the power of the air\" (Ephesians 2:2). Conformed! Somebody else was cutting the shape. It's like a cookie bragging it's one of a kind when three hundred of its cousins came off the exact same stamp.","YES, baker, nobody was flying solo. Paul says they \"conformed to the ways of this world and of the ruler of the power of the air, the spirit who is now at work in the sons of disobedience\" (Ephesians 2:2). That's not one person doing their own thing. That's a whole crowd walking in step, following the same pattern."]},{"t":"story","text":"No, they were going along with the ways of this world and of the {{ruler of the power of the air|ruler of the power of the air}}, the spirit now at work in the {{sons of disobedience|sons of disobedience}}. Then Paul describes a whole way of life: fulfilling the cravings of the {{flesh|flesh}} and {{indulging|indulging}} its desires and thoughts. That's a craving holding the car keys, baker.","ref":"Ephesians 2:2–3"},{"t":"choice","q":"Who does Paul say lived that way at one time, fulfilling the cravings of the {{flesh|flesh}}?","ref":"Ephesians 2:3","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["All of us including Paul","Only the believers in Ephesus","Only the sons of disobedience"],"correct":0,"expl":["THERE it is. Paul writes, \"All of us also lived among them at one time, fulfilling the cravings of our flesh and indulging its desires and thoughts\" (Ephesians 2:3). He doesn't stand outside the oven pointing at everybody else's cookies. He climbs right onto the tray with the whole batch, and honestly, friend, I love him for it.","Paul could've kept the spotlight on them, but listen to how he shifts. Verse 1 says \"you were dead in your trespasses and sins,\" and then verse 3 says \"All of us also lived among them at one time\" (Ephesians 2:1, 3). He goes from \"you\" to \"us\" and drops himself right into the dough. That's the coworker who says, \"Who left the oven on?\" and then sighs, \"Okay. It was me. It was ALL of us.\"","The sons of disobedience are where that spirit \"is now at work\" (Ephesians 2:2). But then Paul writes, \"All of us also lived among them at one time\" (Ephesians 2:3). Among them! Paul isn't pointing across the street at those people, friend. He's saying we had an address on that street too."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then comes the hardest line yet: \"Like the rest, we were by nature {{children of wrath|children of wrath}}.\" That's not one burnt edge, friend, that's the whole batch right from the dough, but then Paul writes \"But\" and God steps in because of His great love for us.","ref":"Ephesians 2:3–4"},{"t":"choice","q":"God steps in for people who were by nature {{children of wrath|children of wrath}}. Paul calls Him a God who is rich in ___.","ref":"Ephesians 2:3–4","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["mercy","wisdom","justice"],"correct":0,"expl":["MERCY, baker! Paul says, \"But because of His great love for us, God, who is rich in mercy,\" steps in (Ephesians 2:4). And He's not a little bit merciful, He's RICH in it. When you're by nature children of wrath (Ephesians 2:3), that's the one account balance you want to hear about.","God is wise, no doubt about it (Romans 16:27), but that's not the word Paul picks here. He writes, \"God, who is rich in mercy\" (Ephesians 2:4). Think about who He's stepping in for: people Paul calls \"by nature children of wrath\" (Ephesians 2:3). Folks in that spot don't need a smarter plan first, friend. They need mercy, and God's got it by the truckload.","I see why that one came to mind, friend, with all that talk of wrath. But Paul writes, \"But because of His great love for us, God, who is rich in mercy,\" (Ephesians 2:4). That little word \"But\" is doing some HEAVY lifting. Right after \"children of wrath\" (Ephesians 2:3), Paul doesn't reach for justice. He reaches for great love and mercy."]},{"t":"story","text":"God doesn't patch us up or hand us a pep talk. He made us alive with Christ.","ref":"Ephesians 2:5"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says God waited until we had cleaned up our lives, and then He made us alive with Christ.","ref":"Ephesians 2:5","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["Oh, I wish I could take credit for cleaning up, friend, but the verse won't let me. Paul says God \"made us alive with Christ even when we were dead in our trespasses\" (Ephesians 2:5). Even when we were DEAD. Dead people don't tidy up. That's like waiting for the cookie dough to roll itself out and hop in the oven.","You got it, baker. Paul says God \"made us alive with Christ even when we were dead in our trespasses\" (Ephesians 2:5). No cleanup first. No \"get your life together and call Me back.\" God moved while we were still dead, and that's the whole point."]},{"t":"story","text":"Then Paul bursts out, \"It is by {{grace|grace}} you have been {{saved|saved}}!\" And he keeps going: God raised us up with Christ.","ref":"Ephesians 2:5–6"},{"t":"choice","q":"After God raised us up with Christ, where does Paul say God seated us?","ref":"Ephesians 2:6","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["With Him in the heavenly realms","With Him on Mount Zion","With Him in Jerusalem"],"correct":0,"expl":["Heavenly realms, friend! Paul says God \"raised us up with Christ and seated us with Him in the heavenly realms in Christ Jesus\" (Ephesians 2:6). Not standing in the back. Not waiting in the lobby. SEATED. Somebody who was dead in verse 1 now has a seat with Christ, and I'm still not over it.","Mount Zion is a big name in the Bible (Psalm 48:2), so I see the pull. But here Paul says God \"seated us with Him in the heavenly realms in Christ Jesus\" (Ephesians 2:6). He doesn't name a mountain at all. Paul's pointing higher than any hilltop, friend.","Jerusalem makes sense as a guess, baker, but Paul doesn't name a city here. He says God \"raised us up with Christ and seated us with Him in the heavenly realms in Christ Jesus\" (Ephesians 2:6). Notice it's \"with Christ\" and \"with Him\" every step of the way. Paul keeps the focus on who we're with, not on a spot on a map."]},{"t":"story","text":"And Paul tells why God did all this, friend: so that in the coming ages He might put something on display.","ref":"Ephesians 2:7"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says God did all this so that in the coming ages He could show off how hard His people worked.","ref":"Ephesians 2:7","qtype":"true_false","answers":["True","False"],"correct":1,"expl":["Ha! If the display was about how hard I worked, friend, they'd need a VERY small display case. Paul says God did it so that \"in the coming ages He might display the surpassing riches of His grace\" (Ephesians 2:7). The thing on show is His grace and His kindness to us in Christ Jesus. We're not the trophy, we're the proof of how kind He is.","Right, baker, nobody's work goes in the spotlight. Paul says God did it so that \"in the coming ages He might display the surpassing riches of His grace, demonstrated by His kindness to us in Christ Jesus\" (Ephesians 2:7). Surpassing riches! That means grace that goes past anything you could stack next to it. God is putting His grace on display, and it's going to run for ages."]},{"t":"story","text":"No, it's the {{surpassing|surpassing}} riches of His {{grace|grace}}, shown by His kindness to us in Christ Jesus. Then that grace line comes back, and this time Paul adds that it's through {{faith|faith}}. Then he says this is not from yourselves.","ref":"Ephesians 2:7–8"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says, \"and this not from yourselves; it is the ___ of God.\"","ref":"Ephesians 2:8","qtype":"fill_blank","answers":["gift","reward","wage"],"correct":0,"expl":["GIFT! Paul says, \"For it is by grace you have been saved through faith, and this not from yourselves; it is the gift of God,\" (Ephesians 2:8). A gift is something you receive, not something you clock in for. Nobody hands you a present and then slides you an invoice, friend.","A reward sounds nice, friend, but a reward means you did something to win it. Paul says, \"and this not from yourselves; it is the gift of God,\" (Ephesians 2:8). Not from yourselves! You don't get a medal for being rescued from the back of the drawer, trust me. Somebody just opens the drawer.","A wage is what you get for putting in the hours, baker, and Paul shuts that door. He says, \"and this not from yourselves; it is the gift of God,\" (Ephesians 2:8). Then he adds right away that it's \"not by works\" (Ephesians 2:9). There's no time card for this one. You can stop looking for the punch clock."]},{"t":"story","text":"And it's not by {{works|works}}, either. Good news for me, friend: I stamped crooked cookies for YEARS in a junk drawer, and if it came down to my work, I'd still be stuck behind the takeout menus.","ref":"Ephesians 2:9"},{"t":"choice","q":"Paul says being {{saved|saved}} is not by {{works|works}}. What does that mean no one can do?","ref":"Ephesians 2:9","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Boast about it","Ask God for it","Thank God for it"],"correct":0,"expl":["That's it, friend. Paul says salvation is \"not by works, so that no one can boast\" (Ephesians 2:9). If you didn't bake it, you can't brag about it. Imagine somebody walking around a party taking bows for a cookie box somebody else filled. NOBODY gets to do that here.","Paul doesn't say anything here about asking, baker. He says it's \"not by works, so that no one can boast\" (Ephesians 2:9). The thing that gets shut down is bragging. Paul calls it \"the gift of God\" (Ephesians 2:8), so nobody gets to strut around like they earned it.","Oh, thanking God is not what's off the table, friend! Paul says it's \"not by works, so that no one can boast\" (Ephesians 2:9). Boasting is what goes. If a gift leaves you with nothing to brag about, about the only thing left to say is thank you."]},{"t":"story","text":"So do good {{works|works}} even matter? Paul says we are God's {{workmanship|workmanship}}, created in Christ Jesus, and let me tell you, baker, God doesn't stamp crooked.","ref":"Ephesians 2:10"},{"t":"choice","q":"We are God's {{workmanship|workmanship}}, created in Christ Jesus. What does Paul say we were created to do?","ref":"Ephesians 2:10","qtype":"multiple_choice","answers":["Good works God prepared in advance","Good works that earn His grace","Good works that pay off our sins"],"correct":0,"expl":["BEAUTIFUL, baker. Paul says we are \"created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance as our way of life\" (Ephesians 2:10). He prepared them in ADVANCE. Good works aren't how we get in the door. God lays them out ahead of time, like a baker who sets every cutter on the counter before the dough is even ready.","Can't earn a gift, friend, Paul just told us that. Grace saves \"through faith, and this not from yourselves; it is the gift of God, not by works\" (Ephesians 2:8-9). Then he says we're \"created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance as our way of life\" (Ephesians 2:10). Good works don't come before grace to earn it. They come after, because God made us for them.","Paul never sends a bill, baker. He says we're \"created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance as our way of life\" (Ephesians 2:10). Back in verse 5, God made us alive while we were dead in our trespasses, and Paul says, \"It is by grace you have been saved!\" (Ephesians 2:5). The good works are our way of life now, not a payment plan."]}]},{"id":"b","kind":"burnt","title":"Burnt Cookies","intro":"One last retelling, friend. Two of these cookies stayed in my oven too long, so each one has a detail that's wrong. Tap the 2 burnt ones.","lines":[{"text":"Paul starts with where the believers in Ephesus used to be: dead in their trespasses and sins, walking in them, going along with the ways of this world and the ruler of the power of the air. Not napping, friend. DEAD.","ref":"Ephesians 2:1–2","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"And Paul says all of us lived that way once, fulfilling the cravings of the flesh. By nature we were children of the devil. But God, rich in mercy, steps in because of His great love for us.","ref":"Ephesians 2:3–4","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"children of the devil","true":"children of wrath","ref":"Ephesians 2:3"}]},{"text":"God made us alive with Christ even when we were dead in our trespasses. \"It is by grace you have been saved!\" Then He raised us up with Christ and seated us with Him in the heavenly realms. Front-row seat, baker, and I never even bought a ticket.","ref":"Ephesians 2:5–6","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"Why? So that in the coming ages He can display the surpassing riches of His grace. We're saved by grace through faith. It's the gift of God, not by works, so no one can boast. Nobody's bragging about their batch on this one.","ref":"Ephesians 2:7–9","burnt":false,"wrong":[]},{"text":"We are God's temple, created in Christ Jesus to do good works that God prepared in advance as our way of life. And God doesn't stamp crooked.","ref":"Ephesians 2:10","burnt":true,"wrong":[{"wrong":"God's temple","true":"God's workmanship","ref":"Ephesians 2:10"}]}]},{"id":"e","kind":"ending","title":"The Big Idea","lesson":"","bigIdea":["Here's the whole batch, baker. We didn't start out a little off. Paul says we were \"dead in our trespasses\" (Eph 2:5). We walked in the ways of this world and of the ruler of the power of the air, gave our flesh whatever it craved, and were by nature children of wrath (Eph 2:1-3).","And dead means DEAD, friend. A dead guy can't fix his life. He can't even hit the snooze button.","So the rescue had to be all God. Out of His rich mercy and great love, He made dead people alive with Christ, and we brought nothing to it but the need (Eph 2:4-5). Then He raised us up with Christ and seated us with Him in the heavenly realms, so that in the coming ages He might display the surpassing riches of His grace, shown in His kindness to us in Christ Jesus (Eph 2:6-7).","That's grace. Being saved is God's gift, received through faith, not a paycheck for our works, so nobody gets to brag (Eph 2:8-9).","Think about it. Nobody brags about how hard they worked to open a present.","And good works? They're not how we get saved. They're what God made us in Christ Jesus to do (Eph 2:10).","Grace first, works second. Flip that order and you're frosting the cookie before it's even baked. And Paul's not done with this letter yet, friend."]}],"words":{"apostle":{"word":"apostle","meaning":"Someone Jesus chose and sent out with His authority to tell people about Him and to start and lead churches.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"trespasses":{"word":"trespasses","meaning":"Wrong things a person does by stepping over the line God has drawn. It is close in meaning to sins.","sources":["Strong's Concordance"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"walked":{"word":"walked","meaning":"Here it means lived or behaved, as a habit. To walk in sins is to make them your everyday way of life.","sources":["Strong's Concordance"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"ruler of the power of the air":{"word":"ruler of the power of the air","meaning":"A title for the devil, Satan, who leads the evil spiritual forces working unseen in the world.","sources":["Matthew Henry's Commentary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"sons of disobedience":{"word":"sons of disobedience","meaning":"People whose lives are marked by refusing to obey God. Calling them \"sons\" of it means it shapes who they are.","sources":["Matthew Henry's Commentary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"flesh":{"word":"flesh","meaning":"Here it does not mean skin and muscle. It means human nature turned away from God, which pulls a person toward sin.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"indulging":{"word":"indulging","meaning":"Giving in to something you want instead of holding back.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"children of wrath":{"word":"children of wrath","meaning":"People who, because of the sinful nature they are born with, stand under God's holy anger against sin and deserve His judgment.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary","Matthew Henry's Commentary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"mercy":{"word":"mercy","meaning":"Kindness that holds back the punishment someone deserves and helps them instead.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"grace":{"word":"grace","meaning":"God's favor and kindness given freely to people who have not earned it and could never earn it.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"saved":{"word":"saved","meaning":"Rescued by God from sin and from the punishment it brings, and brought into new life with Him.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"heavenly realms":{"word":"heavenly realms","meaning":"The unseen world of heaven, where God is and where Christ reigns, as opposed to the earthly world we see.","sources":["Strong's Concordance"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"surpassing":{"word":"surpassing","meaning":"Going far beyond, greater than anything it could be measured against.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"faith":{"word":"faith","meaning":"Trusting in God and relying on Christ, not on yourself, to be made right with Him.","sources":["Easton's Bible Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"works":{"word":"works","meaning":"Things a person does; deeds. Here, the good things people might do hoping to earn God's approval.","sources":["Strong's Concordance"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"boast":{"word":"boast","meaning":"To brag, or to take pride in something as though you earned it yourself.","sources":["Webster's 1828 Dictionary"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"},"workmanship":{"word":"workmanship","meaning":"Something carefully made by a skilled maker. Paul means God crafted us on purpose, like a work of art.","sources":["Strong's Concordance"],"kindLabel":"Word on the path"}}}];
